/* The Project To-Do checklist seed.
 *
 * MOVED OUT OF admin.html 2026-08-14. It is ~217KB — 12.8% of the page —
 * and was downloaded on every admin load and pushed down the wire again on
 * every deploy, even though the test list changes far less often than the
 * dashboard wrapped around it. admin.html now pulls it in with a dynamic
 * import, inside runProjectTestSync, so it is fetched only when the checklist
 * actually syncs rather than on every sign-in.
 *
 * Each row is [id, area, title, steps, expected, version, status, notes,
 * retestReason]. Bump the version whenever the wording changes, or the test
 * quietly goes stale — see CLAUDE.md §0 and §9.9.
 */
export const TEST_SEED = [
  [1,"Public Site","Quote request form","1. Open a new tab in your web browser and go to highlightingutah.com (this is the public website that customers see — don't log into anything).\n2. Near the top of the page, find and click the gold button that says 'Free Quote'.\n3. A form will pop up. Fill in every box: your name, phone number, email, street address, city, zip code, and how you'd like to be contacted (phone call, text, or email).\n4. Click the 'Submit' button at the bottom of the form.\n5. Now open the admin site (the private office dashboard — ask for the login if you don't have it) and look at the menu on the left side of the screen.\n6. Click the words 'Quote Requests'.","On the public website, you should see a thank-you message appear. In admin's Quote Requests list, a new card should appear at the very top with everything you just typed in it, and a small label that says 'New'.",1,"Pass","",""],
  [2,"Public Site","Quick quote widget","1. Open a new tab in your web browser and go to highlightingutah.com (the public website that customers see — don't log into anything).\n2. Scroll down the entire homepage from the very top to the very bottom, looking at everything on the page as you go.\n3. Look for a short quote box somewhere on the page that only asks for a name and one way to contact you (like a phone number or email) — this is different from the full quote form with lots of boxes that you may have already tested separately.\n4. If you find a short quote box like that, fill in the name box and the contact box.\n5. Click the button to submit it.\n6. Open the admin site (the private office dashboard) and look at the menu on the left side of the screen.\n7. Click the words 'Quote Requests'.","If you found a short quote box on the homepage, it should let you fill in just a name and a way to contact you, and after you submit it, a new entry with what you typed should appear in admin's Quote Requests list. If there is no short quote box anywhere on the homepage, write down 'N/A' for this test instead of a pass or fail.",1,"N/A","Could not find a quick quote widget on the site.",""],
  [3,"Public Site","Reviews / gallery / FAQ","1. Open a new tab in your web browser and go to highlightingutah.com (the public website).\n2. Scroll down the page until you find the reviews section.\n3. Keep scrolling until you find the photo gallery section.\n4. Keep scrolling until you find the FAQ (Frequently Asked Questions) section.\n5. Open the admin site (the private office dashboard) and look at the menu on the left side of the screen, under the heading 'Websites'.\n6. Click the word 'Reviews' and write down every review listed there.\n7. Click the word 'Gallery' and write down every photo listed there.\n8. Click the words 'Hero Images' and write down every image listed there.\n9. Click the word 'FAQ' and write down every question and answer listed there.","Every review, photo, hero image, and FAQ question/answer that is listed in admin's Reviews, Gallery, Hero Images, and FAQ pages should also be showing on the public website's reviews section, photo gallery, and FAQ section. Nothing that is in admin should be missing from the public site, and nothing should be showing on the public site that isn't in admin.",1,"Pass","",""],
  [4,"Public Site","Contact message","1. Open a new tab in your web browser and go to highlightingutah.com (the public website).\n2. Find the contact form somewhere on the site.\n3. In the name box, type a name you will recognize later, like 'TEST MESSAGE'.\n4. Fill in the rest of the contact form and type a message.\n5. Send the message.\n6. Open the admin site (the private office dashboard) and look at the menu on the left side of the screen.\n7. Click the words 'Customer Messages'.","Your test message should be sitting in the Inbox inside Customer Messages, showing the same name (like 'TEST MESSAGE') and the same message text you typed and sent.",1,"Pass","",""],
  [5,"Member Portal","Sign in (phone)","1. Open a new tab in your web browser and go to highlightingutah.com (the public website).\n2. Near the top of the page, find and click 'Member Portal' in the top menu (this is the private area where a customer checks their own account).\n3. In the phone number box, type a real customer's phone number.\n4. In the last name box, type that same customer's last name.\n5. Click the 'Sign In' button.","A gold spinner should appear on the screen saying 'Opening your account'. After a moment that spinner should disappear, and the customer's invoice should load along with a row of tabs across the screen.",4,"Pass","","Sign-in errors now say what actually went wrong. If you trip the \"too many attempts\" limit you should be told to wait 15 minutes, not told your name is wrong."],
  [6,"Member Portal","Sign in (email)","1. Go back to the Member Portal (the customer login area on highlightingutah.com that you used in the last test).\n2. If you are still signed in from before, click the button or link to sign out.\n3. This time, in the box where you'd normally type a phone number, type that same customer's email address instead.\n4. In the last name box, type that customer's last name.\n5. Click the 'Sign In' button.\n6. In admin, check that customer's record to see if there is a second email address on file for them.\n7. If there is a second email on file, sign out of the Member Portal again.\n8. Sign in one more time using that second email address along with the customer's last name.","Every time you signed in — no matter which of the customer's email addresses you typed — the account should open exactly the same way: loading their invoice and the row of tabs, just like when you signed in with their phone number.",3,"Pass","","Same page change as the phone sign-in: the demo-number line is gone and failed saves no longer show a debug string."],
  [7,"Member Portal","Loading spinner","1. Open the Member Portal (the customer login area on highlightingutah.com).\n2. Type in a customer's phone number (or email) and last name.\n3. Click the 'Sign In' button.\n4. Without clicking anything else, watch the screen closely.","The spinner should appear right away, as soon as you click Sign In. It should disappear the moment either the account finishes loading or an error message appears — it should never keep spinning forever without anything happening.",1,"Pass","",""],
  [8,"Member Portal","Wrong credentials","AUTOMATED — npm run test:browser (test/portal.spec.js, \"t8 — a wrong last name is refused, and says so\") proves the mechanism on every run: a phone number with a non-matching last name gets refused and the sign-in form reappears, with nothing thrown. What is left for you is the real page: confirm the message still reads warmly (not a raw error code) and nothing looks broken on screen.\n\n1. Open the Member Portal (the customer login area on highlightingutah.com).\n2. In the phone number box, type a real customer's phone number, but in the last name box type a last name that does NOT belong to that customer, so the two don't match.\n3. Click the 'Sign In' button.","A friendly message should appear saying something like 'we couldn't find an account.' The sign-in form should reappear so you can try again. You should NOT see a technical error code, and you should NOT see a blank screen.",3,"Pass","","Wired to the automated regression test (test/portal.spec.js). Reworded to point at what that test can't check — the real page — instead of re-proving the mechanism by hand."],
  [10,"Member Portal","Balance reflects credits","AUTOMATED - the credit maths is proved on every commit by run-all.js (credits reduce what is owed, a credit larger than the bill still reads Paid in Full, one exactly equal to the bill reads Paid in Full, and a balance never goes negative). What is left for you is the VISIBLE half: sign into the portal as a customer with a credit and confirm the balance on screen matches, and that the credit is explained rather than the number just being mysteriously smaller.\n\n1. Open the admin site (the private office dashboard) and look at the menu on the left side of the screen. Click the word 'Invoices'.\n2. Click 'Invoice List'.\n3. Find a customer and add a credit to their invoice — or, if you'd rather not change anything, pick a customer who already has a credit on their invoice.\n4. Write down four numbers from that customer's invoice: the install amount, the removal amount, the credit amount, and the amount they have already paid.\n5. Open the Member Portal (the customer login area on highlightingutah.com) and sign in as that same customer.\n6. Click the 'Payment' tab.\n7. Look at the balance shown there.","The balance shown on the Payment tab should equal: the install amount plus the removal amount, minus the credit, minus whatever the customer has already paid. If you add up install and removal, subtract the credit, and subtract what's already been paid, that final number should match the balance shown in the portal exactly.",4,"N/A","","The credit maths is now automated. Reworded to check only what the customer sees in the portal.  Also: the balance is now itemised — installation, removal, each light-change fee and each credit with its reason, then the total. Check the lines add up to the number shown."],
  [12,"Member Portal","Change light colours","1. Pick a test customer to use for this test — do NOT use a real customer, because changing light colours through the portal charges the customer a $30 fee.\n2. Open the Member Portal (the customer login area on highlightingutah.com) and sign in as that test customer.\n3. Click the 'Light Colors' tab (if you're using a phone, this tab is in the row of tabs across the bottom of the screen).\n4. Change the light colours to something different than what's currently selected.\n5. Click 'Save'.\n6. Open the admin site (the private office dashboard) and look at the menu on the left side of the screen. Click the word 'Customers'.\n7. Click 'All Customers'.\n8. Find that test customer in the list and click 'Edit' on their entry.\n9. Go back to the menu on the left side of the screen and click the word 'Warehouse'.\n10. Click the 'Build' tab.","The portal should show a message confirming the colour change was saved. In admin, the Edit Customer popup for that person should show the new colours you picked. That same customer should now also appear in the Warehouse Build list. They should ALSO now be held off the install routes for 48 hours, so they can keep adjusting - check they do not turn up as available to schedule until that window has passed. And if that test customer was already on a route, a note titled 'Lights Changed After Assignment' should appear in Messages under the System folder, naming them.",3,"Retest","","IMPORTANT — this one was CHARGING PEOPLE WRONGLY. Opening Light Colors and pressing Save without changing anything used to add a $30 fee and delete the pattern note. It should now say \"Nothing changed — no charge\". Also check a real change KEEPS any note in brackets, e.g. \"(every third bulb)\", and that the colour swatches are big enough not to mis-tap. UPDATED 2026-08-21: a real change now also charges $30, opens a 48-hour window during which they are not scheduled, and tells the crew if they were already on a route."],
  [13,"Member Portal","Change contact info","1. Open the Member Portal (the customer login area on highlightingutah.com) and sign in as a test customer.\n2. Click the 'Information' tab.\n3. Change the phone number to a different test value.\n4. Change the gate code to a different test value.\n5. Change the street address to a different test value.\n6. Click 'Save'.\n7. Open the admin site (the private office dashboard) and look at the menu on the left side of the screen. Click the word 'Customers'.\n8. Click 'All Customers'.\n9. Find that test customer and click 'Edit' on their entry.\n10. Go back to the menu on the left side of the screen and click the word 'Routes'.\n11. Find an upcoming saved route that includes that test customer as a stop.","The Edit Customer popup should show the new phone number, gate code, and street address you just typed in the portal. The upcoming route stop for that customer should also show the new gate code and the new address.",1,"Pass","",""],
  [16,"Member Portal","Request cancellation","1. Open the Member Portal (the customer login area on highlightingutah.com) and sign in as a test customer.\n2. Click the 'Cancel' tab.\n3. In the reason box, type a reason you'll recognize later.\n4. Submit the cancellation.\n5. Open the admin site (the private office dashboard) and look at the menu on the left side of the screen. Click the word 'Customers'.\n6. Click 'All Customers'.\n7. Find that test customer's card in the list.","That test customer's card in admin should show the words 'Cancellation Requested', and the reason you typed should be readable somewhere on that card.",1,"Pass","",""],
  [18,"Admin","Quotes - view & filter","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard — not highlightingutah.com, which is the public site customers see).\n2. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen. Click the words \"Quote Requests\".\n3. Pick any one quote card in the list and read it from the top all the way to the bottom.\n4. Near the top of the page, find the filter control.\n5. Use the filter to switch it to \"New only\".\n6. Use the filter to switch it to show all quotes.","Each quote card should show the customer's name, its status, their address, their contact details, and controls for adding or managing a photo. When you switch the filter to 'New only', the list should get shorter, showing fewer cards. When you switch it back to show all quotes, the list should get longer again.",1,"Pass","",""],
  [19,"Admin","Quotes - price a quote","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \"Quote Requests\".\n3. Find a quote card marked \"New\" that is one of your own test submissions (not a real customer's).\n4. On that card, find the box called \"Estimated Feet\" and type a number into it.\n5. Find the box called \"Quoted Price\" and type a price into it.\n6. Click anywhere outside that box (or press the Tab key). The price saves by itself the moment you click out, and a small message pops up confirming it - there is no Save button to press.\n7. Find and click the button called \"Send quote email\" - this shows you a link plus a filled-in email right below the button.\n8. The card now sits under the \"Awaiting Response\" tab. Find it there and open its \"Pricing & Approval\" panel again.\n9. Type a different, higher price into the \"Quoted Price\" box - as if the customer had just called and asked you to add another part of the house - then click outside the box.\n10. Refresh the whole page in your browser, then open that card's \"Pricing & Approval\" panel one more time and read the price.\n11. Copy the link shown in that box and open it in your browser, as if you were the customer. This link ends in &action=approve.\n12. Now go and open the email itself, the one that actually landed in the customer's inbox, and press the big gold \"View Your Quote\" button at the bottom of it. That button carries a DIFFERENT link - just a token, with no &action on the end - and it is the one most customers press.\n13. On that page, find and press the button to approve.","The feet and price you typed should save on the card on their own, without pressing any Save button. Clicking \"Send quote email\" should show a link and an editable email underneath it, and the card should move to \"Awaiting Response\". The higher price you typed in step 9 must still be there after the refresh in step 10 - it must NOT snap back to the old price - and the customer's page at that link should show that new higher price too.\n\nBOTH links must let the customer answer. The one from the card (step 11) drops them straight into the install-details form, because pressing that link IS approving. The \"View Your Quote\" button from the email (step 12) does not say which answer they meant, so that page should now ask: \"Your Highlighting Utah quote\" with three buttons - Approve my quote, Maybe next year, Not right now. Pressing Approve there should take them into the same details form, and the quote should show as approved back in admin.",4,"Retest","The approval link works but the accept-my-quote button does not.","FIXED, 2026-08-18 - this is what that note was about. There are two shapes of quote link and only one of them worked. The coloured buttons inside the email body carry &action=approve and were fine. The big \"View Your Quote\" button at the bottom of every quote and nudge email, and the link in both TEXT MESSAGE templates, are a bare token with no action - and the site did nothing at all with those. The customer landed on the install-details form, headed \"Thanks for approving!\" when they had not approved, with no approve button anywhere and no way to submit the form either. That page now asks which of the three they meant. Steps 12 and 13 are new and test exactly that. The browser suite covers it too (test/quote-link.spec.js), so what is left for you is the real email in a real inbox on a real phone.\n\nThe Quoted Price box now saves on its own when you click out of it. It used to save ONLY if you went on to press the send button, so changing the price on a quote that had already gone out silently reverted. Steps 8-10 are new and test exactly that. The button name is also corrected to \"Send quote email\", which is what it actually says on screen."],
  [20,"Admin","Quotes - detail form","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \"Quote Requests\".\n3. Approve a test quote (one of your own test submissions).\n4. Open the link shown under the \"Send Email\" button for that quote in your browser, as if you were the customer.\n5. On the public site, fill in the customer's own detail form: choose a wire colour, choose an outlet timer, and choose an install month.\n6. Submit that form.\n7. Go back to admin and open \"Quote Requests\" again.\n8. Near the top, find and click the tab called \"Form\".\n9. Find the same quote you just submitted the form for.\n10. Now find a different approved quote where the customer has NOT yet submitted their detail form.","For the quote where the customer submitted the form, the card should show the Wire colour and Timer choices they picked, right on the card itself. For the quote where the customer has NOT submitted the form yet, those lines should simply not appear. There is no separate 'Detail Form' section to open or edit in admin anymore, because customers now fill that form in on their own portal after their quote is approved.",2,"Retest","Probably should not ask which outlet - the crew decides that - but do ask if they have one.","The admin-side Detail Form was removed from the quote card in the P1 rework - it's now filled in on the customer's own portal after they approve, not edited here. This Pass needs a fresh look against how it actually works now."],
  [21,"Admin","Quotes - convert to customer (manual)","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \"Quote Requests\".\n3. Find an approved quote where the customer has already submitted their detail form back to you.\n4. Click the button called \"Convert to Customer\". A popup should appear asking HOW you want to convert them - it did not used to do this, it used to go straight to the form.\n5. Read the popup. Then click the button that says \"Fill in manually\".\n6. Check that all of the following carried across correctly into the form: the light colours, the house areas, the outlet timer, the specific outlet, the install timing, any notes, the photo (with its markup/drawing on it), the approved price, the contact preference, and the \"Charge $30 one-time set-up fee\" tick box.\n7. Type a number into the \"Measured Feet\" box, and while you type, watch both the \"Number of Bins\" box and the line underneath the Customer # box.\n8. Click the button called \"Add Customer\".\n9. Go back to \"Quote Requests\" in the left menu.","The popup should offer three things: \"Convert automatically\", \"Fill in manually\", and Cancel. Clicking \"Fill in manually\" should open the Add a Customer form already filled in - the same thing the button used to do on its own. Every detail listed in step 6 should have carried across, including the $30 set-up fee tick matching what was ticked on the quote. In step 7, the Number of Bins box and the line under Customer # should both update as you type, and they should agree with each other. A new customer record should exist with every one of those details on it, at the price the customer approved. Back in Quote Requests, that quote should now say Converted.",2,"Pass","","Convert to Customer now asks automatic-or-manual first. This test covers the manual half; test 205 covers the automatic half. Also added: the $30 fee and the live Number of Bins box."],
  [22,"Admin","Add Customer - full detail","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \"Customers\".\n3. Click the tab called \"Add a Customer\".\n4. Before filling anything else in, type just a street address and a city, then click the button called \"Add Customer\". Read the red \"Still missing\" box that appears directly above that button, and read the grey message next to the Add Customer button. Note that NO popup appears this time.\n5. Now tick one light colour, and nothing else. Click \"Add Customer\" again. This time a popup should appear listing what is still missing. Click Cancel on it.\n6. Now fill in every single box on the form: name, phone, email, address, light colours, house parts, install timing, house notes, outlet timer, specific outlet, gate code, measured feet, and price.\n7. Upload a house photo.\n8. Highlight (mark up and draw on) that photo.\n9. Click the button called \"Add Customer\".\n10. Click the tab called \"All Customers\".\n11. Find the test customer you just added.\n12. Click \"Edit\" on their record.","At step 4 the red \"Still missing\" box should name all FIVE things left blank at once, rather than one at a time - Phone Number, Email, Light Colors, House Picture and Total Price. No popup should appear this time, and the grey message beside the button should tell you to pick their light colours, because a customer with no colours never reaches the Warehouse to be built. Nothing should be saved.\n\nLight colours are the one thing on this form you cannot skip. Everything else can deliberately be filled in later - the photo gets taken next week, the price is still being agreed - so at step 5, once a colour is ticked, you should finally get the popup, listing the four that are left and offering to add them anyway. Pressing Cancel should leave the form exactly as you left it with nothing saved.\n\nAt the end, a popup window should open showing the customer's information, and every single thing you typed or uploaded in steps 6 through 8 should be there. Nothing should be missing or quietly dropped.",4,"Pass","","Add Customer now lists everything that is missing in one go, in a red box above the button, and can still be overridden on purpose. A house Google cannot place on the map is now saved anyway with a warning, instead of being refused.\n\nUpdated 2026-08-15: LIGHT COLOURS ARE NOW REQUIRED and are the one thing on this form that CANNOT be overridden - the old \"no colours picked, add them anyway?\" prompt is gone. That is what steps 4 and 5 check now, and it is why the step numbers after them shifted by one. The reason: a customer saved with no colours is invisible to the Warehouse, so they were never built for and nothing said so."],
  [24,"Admin","Add Customer - photo markup","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \"Customers\".\n3. Click the tab called \"Add a Customer\".\n4. Upload a house photo.\n5. Click the button called \"Mark Up\" (the tool for drawing on the photo).\n6. Using the red drawing tool, draw a red line on the photo.\n7. Using the yellow pen tool, draw something else on the photo.\n8. Click the button to save the markup.\n9. Finish filling in the form and click \"Add Customer\".\n10. Click the tab called \"All Customers\".\n11. Find the test customer you just added and click \"Edit\" on their record.","The photo should still be there, and it should still show both your red line and your yellow pen mark on it. The markup should have saved along with the record.",1,"Pass","",""],
  [25,"Admin","Edit Customer - edit every field","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \"Customers\".\n3. Click the tab called \"All Customers\".\n4. Find a test customer and click \"Edit\" on their record.\n5. Change a little bit of every one of these fields: name, phone, email, address, light colours, house areas, install timing, gate code, outlet, notes, measured feet, price, bins, wire colour, and RSVP (whether they said yes or no to having lights this year).\n6. Click the button called \"Save\".\n7. Reopen the same \"Edit\" popup for that same customer.\n8. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \"Warehouse\".\n9. Click the tab called \"Build\".","Every single change you made in step 5 should still be there when you reopen the Edit popup, and nothing else on the record should have been erased or reset. Also, because you changed the light colours, that same customer should now appear in the Warehouse Build list.",1,"Pass","",""],
  [26,"Admin","Edit Customer - photo + markup here","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \"Customers\".\n3. Click the tab called \"All Customers\".\n4. Find a customer who already has a house photo on their record, and click \"Edit\" on them.\n5. Replace their photo with a different photo.\n6. Click the button called \"Highlight\" or \"Mark Up Photo\".\n7. Draw on the new photo.\n8. Click the button called \"Save\".\n9. Reopen the same \"Edit\" popup for that same customer.","The new photo you uploaded, along with the markup you drew, should both be saved and visible when you reopen the Edit popup. Also keep in mind that this Edit popup should be the only place in the app where photos can be edited — you should not be able to change or mark up a photo from the route card or from the invoice.",1,"Pass","",""],
  [27,"Admin","Edit Customer - house price to invoice","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Pick a customer who pays only for their own house, not someone who is billed for and covers several houses at once.\n3. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \"Invoices\".\n4. Click \"Invoice List\".\n5. Find that customer and write down their current invoice amount.\n6. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \"Customers\".\n7. Click the tab called \"All Customers\".\n8. Find the same customer and click \"Edit\" on their record.\n9. Change their house price.\n10. Click \"Save\".\n11. Go back to \"Invoices\", then \"Invoice List\".\n12. Find that same customer again.","The invoice amount should now match the new price you set, not the old one. If that customer already had the $30 new-member fee added to their invoice before you made this change, that $30 fee should still be included on the invoice afterward.",2,"Pass","","Invoices is now its own item in the left menu instead of a tab inside Dashboard - the click-by-click steps were rewritten, so this needs running against the new path."],
  [28,"Admin","Edit Customer - payer group guard","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Find a payer who covers several houses. In admin, this means more than one house is billed to the same phone number.\n3. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \"Invoices\".\n4. Click \"Invoice List\".\n5. Find that payer and write down their invoice total, and write down which houses that invoice covers.\n6. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \"Customers\".\n7. Click the tab called \"All Customers\".\n8. Click \"Edit\" on just one of those houses, not all of them.\n9. Change that one house's price.\n10. Click \"Save\".\n11. Go back to \"Invoices\", then \"Invoice List\".\n12. Find that same payer again.","The invoice total should be recalculated using all of that payer's houses added together, not just the one you changed. Every house that was on the invoice before should still be listed on it — none of the other houses should have disappeared.",3,"N/A","","Payer-group behaviour changed — a multi-house invoice now has editable per-property prices and a read-only total."],
  [32,"Admin","Estimated feet autosaves","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \"Quote Requests\".\n3. Pick any quote card.\n4. Find the box called \"Estimated Feet\" on that card and type a number into it.\n5. Do NOT click any save button.\n6. Click on an empty area somewhere else on the page, not the box you just typed in.\n7. Refresh the whole browser page (reload it).\n8. Find that same quote card again.","The number you typed into Estimated Feet should still be there after the page refreshes. It should have saved itself automatically when you clicked away from the box, even though you never pressed a save button.",1,"","",""],
  [46,"Warehouse","Warehouse extras","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word \"Warehouse\".\n3. Click the tab called \"Build\".\n4. Near the top of the page, find the box labeled \"Add From Inbox\".\n5. Leave the first box - the one asking which house this is for - completely blank.\n6. Leave \"What is this for?\" set to \"Lights\". The Pattern is now PICKED, not typed - there is no text box any more. Click the \"+ Red\" button and then the \"+ Pure White\" button, and check that two numbered chips appear underneath reading \"1. Red\" and \"2. Pure White\". Click \"+ Red\" once more, then click the small x on that third chip to take it off again.\n7. In the \"How many bundles\" box, type the number 3.\n8. Leave the Wire Color box on \"Not set - buffer stock\" for now.\n9. Click the button that says \"Add to Queue\".\n10. Scroll down to the queue below and find the buffer stock group that matches the colours you just picked. Press its \"Show items\" button to open it.\n11. On the row for the entry you just added, click the button that says \"Edit\".\n12. The form at the top of the page should fill in with what you picked - the chips should come back showing \"1. Red\" and \"2. Pure White\" - and its heading should now say \"Edit This Build\". Change the Wire Color to \"Green\", and leave everything else alone.\n13. Click the button that says \"Save Changes\".\n14. Look at the queue again and find your entry - check which group it is sitting in now, and what the small grey line under it says.\n15. Open that group and, in the count box on it, type the number 1 and click \"Mark Completed\".\n16. Look at the queue again and check what that entry now shows.\n17. Complete the remaining 2 of that same entry.","Editing must change the entry you clicked Edit on - it must NOT create a second one, so there should still be only the one entry you made. After saving, the small grey line under it should read \"Green wire\", and it should have moved out of the \"buffer stock (wire not set)\" group into the group for that colour pattern on green wire - alongside any real houses with those same colours on green wire. Then, after completing 1 out of the 3, the entry should still be sitting in the queue showing 2 remaining. It should NOT disappear or get deleted just because 1 was completed. Only after all 3 have been completed should it disappear.\n\nOn the Pattern picker itself: clicking a colour should add it to the end of the list, clicking the same colour twice should give you two chips (that is how a repeating pattern like Warm White, Red, Red, Warm White is built), and the small x on a chip should remove just that one. There should be no way to type into the Pattern field at all.",4,"","","Entries added by hand can now be corrected instead of only deleted - every one of them has an Edit button - and the form now asks for a Wire Color, which it never did before, so anything added by hand could only ever say \"wire not set\". Picking a house fills the wire in from their record, the same way the pattern and the bundle count already do.\n\nUpdated 2026-08-15: the Pattern is now PICKED from colour buttons instead of typed into a text box. That is the change to check this time - steps 6 and 12, and the note at the end of what to expect. The reason is that whatever was typed became the heading the whole build queue is grouped under, so one stray spelling made a whole extra group with its own bundle count."],
  [50,"Warehouse","Build-ahead goals","1. On a phone, open the Crew Portal (the app/site the installation crew uses).\n2. Go to the section called \"Warehouse\".\n3. Set a build-ahead goal by entering a colour pattern and how many of that pattern you want built.\n4. Record some progress against that goal (enter a number showing how many have been built so far).\n5. Close the Crew Portal completely.\n6. Reopen the Crew Portal.","The build-ahead goal and the progress you recorded should both still be there after reopening the portal - nothing should have reset. Note this known gap: there is currently no way to see or set these build-ahead goals from admin at all; they only exist inside the Crew Portal.",1,"","",""],
  [62,"Routes","Calendar, Map and Take Downs views","1. Log into admin.\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Routes'.\n3. Click the tab labeled 'List View'.\n4. Click the tab labeled 'Generate Route'.\n5. Click the tab labeled 'Calendar'.\n6. Click the tab labeled 'Map View'.\n7. Click the tab labeled 'Take Downs'.\n8. Pick one route that has already been saved.\n9. Look for that same route in the List View tab.\n10. Look for that same route in the Calendar tab.\n11. Look for that same route in the Map View tab.\n12. Look for that same route in the Take Downs tab (if it applies to that type of route).","All five tabs (List View, Generate Route, Calendar, Map View, Take Downs) should open without any error messages. The same saved route you picked should be findable across the relevant views — they're all showing the same underlying routes, just displayed in different ways (a list, a calendar, a map, and so on).",1,"","",""],
  [63,"Routes","Reorder route tabs","1. Log into admin.\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Routes'.\n3. Look at the row of tabs (List View, Generate Route, Calendar, Map View, Take Downs).\n4. Click and hold on the 'Calendar' tab, then drag it to a different position in that row, then let go.\n5. Refresh the whole page in your browser (reload it).\n6. Check the order of the tabs again.\n7. Sign out of admin completely.\n8. Sign back into admin.\n9. Click 'Routes' again and check the tab order one more time.","The new tab order you set by dragging the 'Calendar' tab should still be there after refreshing the page, and it should still be there after signing out and signing back in. This means the tab order is saved to your personal login, not just remembered by the computer or browser you happen to be using.",1,"","",""],
  [64,"Crew Portal","Staff login then pick your name","1. On a phone, open a web browser and go to the Crew Portal website (employee.html — this is the private site crew members use, different from the public highlightingutah.com site and different from admin).\n2. Sign in using the shared staff login (the one login/password that all crew members use to get into the Crew Portal).\n3. After signing in, you will see a list of employee names. Tap your own name (or the test employee's name) from that list.","The portal should open as that person, with their name showing and only the tabs that person is allowed to see visible. No other tabs should appear.",2,"","","The \"who are you\" screen is now a list of names to tap instead of a text box. Typing a first name used to match the FIRST person with that name — two people called Jose and the second became the first, pay rate included."],
  [65,"Crew Portal","Role template controls tabs","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard — not the public website, and not the Crew Portal).\n2. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen. Click the word 'Responsibilities'.\n3. Near the top of the Responsibilities page, click the tab that says 'Role Templates'.\n4. Create a new template. Turn on (check) some of the tab checkboxes and leave others off (unchecked) — this template decides which tabs a crew member can see in the Crew Portal.\n5. Still in the Responsibilities section, click the tab that says 'Employee Names'.\n6. Find a test employee and apply (assign) the template you just created to them.\n7. Open the Crew Portal (employee.html, a separate website crew members use) on a phone or computer.\n8. Sign in as that same test employee.","In the Crew Portal, that employee should see only the tabs that were turned on in the template you created. Every tab you left off in the template should not appear at all.",1,"","",""],
  [66,"Crew Portal","Per-employee tab visibility","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word 'Responsibilities'.\n3. Click the tab that says 'Employee Names'.\n4. Click on one employee to open their individual settings.\n5. Tick some of the individual tab checkboxes on and untick others off, just for this one person.\n6. Click 'Save'.\n7. Open the Crew Portal (employee.html, a separate website for crew members) and sign in as that same employee.","That employee should see only the exact tabs you ticked on in step 5, and nothing else. Also check that no other employee's tab access changed because of this — everyone else should still see whatever they saw before.",1,"","",""],
  [67,"Crew Portal","Today's Route loads","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Create and save an install route for today's date, assigned to a specific crew (a group of employees who work together).\n3. On a phone, open the Crew Portal (employee.html) and sign in as one of the employees on that crew.\n4. Tap the button or tab labeled 'Today's Route'.","The list of stops (houses) for the route should load in the same order the office set for the route. Everything on the screen should be easy to read and easy to tap with a finger, and you should not need to pinch to zoom in or scroll sideways to see anything.",2,"","","IMPORTANT — removal routes now appear in the Crew Portal. They never did before, so every day of January showed \"No route scheduled for this day\". Check a removal day loads and is headed Take Downs."],
  [68,"Crew Portal","Live fields re-read","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Create and save a route for today's date that includes a test customer.\n3. On a phone, open the Crew Portal (employee.html) and go to 'Today's Route'.\n4. Find that same test customer's stop and read what it currently shows.\n5. Go back to admin (you can use a computer for this part).\n6. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Customers'.\n7. Click 'All Customers'.\n8. Find that same test customer and click 'Edit'.\n9. Change the house notes to something new.\n10. Change (upload a new) photo for that house.\n11. Click 'Save'.\n12. On the phone, refresh (reload) the 'Today's Route' page in the Crew Portal.","After refreshing, the crew member should see the brand-new note and the brand-new photo on that stop, even though the rest of that stop's information stays the same as when the route was first saved (the route itself is 'frozen' at the time it was made, but notes, outlet info, fix notes, and photos always show the latest, current version).",1,"","",""],
  [69,"Crew Portal","Mark done","1. Open the Crew Portal (employee.html) and go to 'Today's Route'.\n2. Find a house on the route and mark it 'Done' (a button or checkbox labeled Done).\n3. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n4. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Customers'.\n5. Click 'All Customers'.\n6. Find that same house in the list.","That house should show as completed (Done) in admin. This test matters more than it looks — the automatic nightly billing only sends an invoice for houses that are marked Done, so if this does not work, customers will not get billed.",2,"","","Mark Done now writes the correct field for the kind of route: an install marks the house installed, a REMOVAL marks it taken down. This matters for billing — check that marking a removal done does NOT re-invoice the customer."],
  [70,"Crew Portal","Needs fix with note and photo","1. Open the Crew Portal (employee.html) and go to 'Today's Route'.\n2. Find a house and flag it as needing a fix (a repair — something broken that needs a follow-up visit).\n3. Type a note describing the problem.\n4. Attach a photo of the problem.\n5. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n6. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Customers'.\n7. Click 'All Customers'.\n8. Find that same house and open it.\n9. Also, in the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Routes'.\n10. Click 'Generate Route'.\n11. Click the tab labeled 'Fixes'.","On that house's page in admin, you should be able to see the fix note and the photo you just added. Also, when you generate a Fixes route, that same house should now appear in the list of houses needing a fix.",2,"","","Flagging an issue now asks what is wrong and refuses an empty answer, and the note shows on the card. A flag holds up that customer’s whole invoice, so it needs a reason."],
  [71,"Crew Portal","One-time note clears on done","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Find a test house and add a 'one-time note' — a note meant to apply only to this single upcoming visit, not to every future visit.\n3. Open the Crew Portal (employee.html) and go to 'Today's Route'.\n4. Find that test house's stop and confirm the one-time note is showing on it.\n5. Mark that house 'Done'.\n6. Go back to admin and open that same house's page again.","The one-time note should now be gone (cleared, empty) — it was only supposed to apply to that one visit, so it should not still be there and should not show up again on the customer's next visit.",1,"","",""],
  [72,"Crew Portal","Timers tab","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Customers'.\n3. Click 'All Customers'.\n4. Click 'Edit' on one test customer, set the 'Outlet Timer' setting to 'Yes', and click 'Save'.\n5. Click 'Edit' on a second test customer, set the 'Outlet Timer' setting to 'No', and click 'Save'.\n6. Open the Crew Portal (employee.html) and go to the tab labeled 'Timers'.","The customer you set to 'Yes' should appear in the Timers list, and the customer you set to 'No' should NOT appear in that list. The Timers list should only ever contain houses whose Outlet Timer setting is 'Yes'.",1,"","",""],
  [73,"Crew Portal","Clock in and out","1. Open the Crew Portal (employee.html) and sign in as a test employee.\n2. Tap 'My Timecard'.\n3. Tap the button to clock in.\n4. Wait a few minutes.\n5. Tap the button to clock out.\n6. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n7. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Time Logs'.\n8. Click the tab labeled 'Time Log History'.","The shift you just worked should be listed with the correct employee name, the correct start (clock-in) time, the correct end (clock-out) time, and the correct total length of the shift (matching roughly how many minutes passed between clocking in and out).",1,"","",""],
  [74,"Crew Portal","Daily checklist","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Responsibilities'.\n3. Click the tab labeled 'Daily Responsibilities'.\n4. Make sure a checklist exists for the role (job type) that your test employee has.\n5. Open the Crew Portal (employee.html) and sign in as that test employee.\n6. Tap the tab labeled 'Checklist'.\n7. Tick off (check) a few of the items on the checklist.\n8. Fully close the Crew Portal (close the browser tab or app completely, not just navigate to another page).\n9. Reopen the Crew Portal and sign in again as that same test employee.","The items you ticked off should still be ticked, for that same employee and that same day. Also check that those same ticks do not show up as checked when a different employee opens their own checklist — each person's checkmarks should be separate.",1,"","",""],
  [75,"Crew Portal","Time-off request","1. Open the Crew Portal (employee.html) and sign in as a test employee.\n2. Find and use the option to submit a time-off request.\n3. Fill in the dates you want off.\n4. Fill in a reason.\n5. Submit the request.\n6. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n7. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Responsibilities'.\n8. Click the tab labeled 'Time Off'.","The time-off request should be listed there with the correct employee name, the correct dates, and the correct reason you typed in.",1,"","",""],
  [76,"Crew Portal","Note to the office","1. Open the Crew Portal (employee.html) and sign in as a test employee.\n2. Find the option to send a note to the office and send one.\n3. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n4. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Responsibilities', and also check 'Customer Messages' — look in both places to find where crew notes show up.","The note you sent should show up in admin, attached to (labeled with) the correct employee's name — the one who actually sent it.",1,"","",""],
  [77,"Crew Portal","Warehouse crew views","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Make sure there is at least one house sitting in the Warehouse Build list (houses that need light bundles built for them).\n3. Make sure there is at least one house sitting in the Recycle list (houses whose lights need to be taken back and reused).\n4. Open the Crew Portal (employee.html) and sign in as an employee who has warehouse access.\n5. Open the 'Warehouse' section.\n6. Go through the build list and check the houses on it.\n7. Go through the recycle list and check the houses on it.\n8. Go through the timers list and check the houses on it.\n9. Go through the pull lists and check the houses on it.","Each of the four lists (build, recycle, timers, pull) should load with the correct, right houses on it. Also confirm you can actually take action on items from the phone — for example, marking a house as 'built' or marking it as 'recycled' — and that the action actually works.",2,"","","The crew route screen changed: finished stops collapse, there is a \"Hide finished stops\" toggle, a phone link on each stop, and an offline badge in the top bar."],
  [94,"Invoices","Invoice list and filters","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click 'Invoices'.\n3. Click the 'Invoice List' tab.\n4. Wait for the full list to finish loading.\n5. Pick two or three invoices from the list and check their totals against the actual customer prices for those same customers.\n6. Use the filter options near the top of the screen to narrow down the list.","Every invoice in the list should load with the correct total matching the customer's price. Each filter option should actually shorten the list down to only the invoices matching what that filter says it does.",2,"","","Invoices is now its own item in the left menu instead of a tab inside Dashboard - the click-by-click steps were rewritten, so this needs running against the new path."],
  [95,"Invoices","Import / Export","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click 'Invoices'.\n3. Click the 'Import / Export' tab.\n4. Click the option to export to Excel.\n5. Open the downloaded Excel file.\n6. Pick five rows from the file.\n7. Click the 'Invoice List' tab and find those same five customers' invoice cards.\n8. Compare each of the five rows in the file against its matching invoice card.","Every number in the exported Excel file should match what is shown on the invoice cards - no rows should be missing, and no totals should be different between the file and the cards.",4,"","","The invoice export now has its own Tips column. Tips are real money charged to a card and were missing from every export, so anything handed to a tax preparer understated income."],
  [96,"Invoices","Season billing emails (retired)","RETIRED - there is nothing left to test here. The Season Billing Emails tab was removed when Invoices became its own item in the left menu. The same job is done in Automation Emails > Templates: write the wording as a template in the Billing folder and use Send to Selected. Score this one N/A.","Nothing to check. Mark it N/A - the screen it covered no longer exists. The row is kept rather than deleted so the history of why it went away stays readable.",1,"","",""],
  [102,"Fees & Locks","Portal warns before charging","1. Sign in to the Member Portal (the website customers use to manage their own account) as a test customer who is currently outside their free window (meaning a colour change right now would cost $30).\n2. Click the tab called 'Light Colors'.\n3. Start changing the colours.\n4. Before clicking 'Save', read whatever message appears on the screen.\n5. Click 'Save' (or whatever button confirms the change) to confirm.\n6. Read the message the portal shows after you confirm.","Before you confirm, the portal should show a warning that tells you this change will cost $30 and ask you to confirm before it charges you. After you confirm, the portal should tell you the exact date and time the new free window will end. IMPORTANT - the message afterwards must match where the money actually went. If their invoice for this season has NOT been sent yet, it should say the $30 was added to their balance, and their invoice should show a $30 light-change line. If their invoice HAS already been sent, it must instead say the charge is going onto NEXT season's bill - and their current invoice total must NOT move, because nothing re-opens a bill once it has gone out. A message saying 'added to your balance' on a total that has not changed is the one thing that must not happen here.",2,"","","The wording now has two versions because the money has two destinations. Re-read this one on a customer who has ALREADY been invoiced, which is the case that used to be impossible to get right."],
  [204,"Fees & Locks","Nobody is charged the join fee twice","⚠ THIS TEST NEVER PRESSES START NEW SEASON. It only uses the Check First button, which writes nothing at all. Do not press Start New Season to do this test.\n1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Click 'Customers' in the menu on the left, then scroll down to the Danger Zone.\n3. Find 'Start New Season' and press the 'Check First' button next to it. This is a dry run - it changes nothing.\n4. Read the report that appears, in particular the line about customers who stop counting as new members.\n5. Compare that number against roughly how many people you actually took on through quotes this season.\n6. Close the panel WITHOUT pressing Start New Season.","The report should include a line saying how many customers stop counting as new members, and that nobody will be charged the one-time $30 join fee again next season. That number should be about the number of people who joined through a quote this season - not zero, and not the whole book. If it says the whole book, stop and ask, because that would mean people who joined years ago are still marked as new. If there is no such line at all, nobody is being retired from new-member status and the join fee will be charged again next year.",1,"","","NEW 2026-08-21, and it is the human half of a real overcharge that has been fixed. The $30 join fee is one-time, but nothing ever cleared the new-member flag on the customer while the season reset released the guard on the invoice - so it would have been charged again every year, quietly, for as long as somebody stayed a customer. The suite proves the flag is now cleared and that the dry run reports it; what it cannot check is whether the NUMBER is right for your actual book, which is all this row is asking you to eyeball."],
  [205,"Routes","Ticking a house in Schedule marks the customer and bills them","⚠ USE A TEST CUSTOMER. Ticking a real one will invoice them tonight.\n1. Log into admin, open Schedule, and pick a day with your test customer on it.\n2. Note whether their invoice has already been sent (open Invoices and look them up first).\n3. Tick the checkbox beside that one house. It should NOT ask you anything.\n4. Go to Routes, find that customer, and look at their Mark Done button.\n5. Go to Customers, All Customers, find them and open the status dropdown on their row.\n6. Now press the day's All done button. Read the message that appears BEFORE agreeing.\n7. Agree to it, then check a second house on that day the same way.\n8. Leave it until after 7pm Mountain, then check whether the invoice email actually arrived.\n9. Untick a house whose invoice has ALREADY been sent, and read what it says.\n10. Now go to Routes and mark a DIFFERENT house complete there (not in Schedule).\n11. Go back to Schedule, open that same day, and look at that house.\n12. Then untick that house on their customer row in All Customers, and look at Schedule again.","Ticking one house must mark THAT customer complete everywhere - the Routes stop should show Done, and the customer row should show Install Complete ticked. It should not ask you anything for a single tick. 'All done' must ask once before it does anything, name how many houses it is about to mark, and say plainly that anyone whose whole job is finished will be invoiced automatically tonight; pressing Cancel there must change nothing at all. The invoice must actually arrive that night - this is the whole point, and until 2026-08-21 ticking in Schedule wrote nothing to the customer, so working the season off this screen billed nobody. Unticking a house that has already been invoiced must warn you first that unticking cannot un-send the invoice they were already sent. If a stop is not linked to a customer, ticking it should SAY so rather than looking like it worked. At steps 10-12 the traffic must run the OTHER way too: a house marked complete anywhere else - Routes, or the customer row - must already show as ticked when you open Schedule, without you touching anything, and must go back to unticked when you undo it there. Schedule reads the customer now rather than keeping its own answer, so the two can no longer disagree. A house from an imported file that was never matched to a customer keeps whatever Schedule already had, which is correct.",2,"Retest","","UPDATED 2026-08-21 (Job 3): Schedule now READS done from the customer as well as writing it, so steps 10-12 were added - that is the half that used to drift silently. Schedule can now trigger billing, which it never could before. The automated suite proves the writes happen and that Cancel writes nothing; what it cannot prove is that the invoice really lands in their inbox at 7pm, which is the half that matters."],
  [206,"Routes","A takedown being done does not mean the install is undone","1. Use a test customer whose install is already marked complete.\n2. In Schedule, open the Takedowns tab and find that customer on a takedown day.\n3. Tick their takedown.\n4. Go to Customers, All Customers, and open the status dropdown on their row.\n5. Check BOTH boxes on that dropdown - Install Complete and Removal Done.\n6. Now untick Install Complete on that dropdown and press Apply.\n7. Re-open the dropdown and look at Removal Done.\n8. Check nothing new was invoiced overnight.","Ticking the takedown must mark Removal Done and must NOT touch Install Complete - they are two different jobs on the same house. At step 6, unticking Install Complete must leave Removal Done exactly as it was. Until 2026-08-21 that dropdown silently cleared the removal whenever the install was unticked, and it also greyed the Removal box out whenever the install was not ticked - both are gone, so you should be able to tick Removal Done on its own. Nothing should be re-invoiced by any of this: a takedown is not a new install.",1,"","","NEW 2026-08-21. Owner: 'Schedules should mark routes as complete automatically. However that does not mean takedown is done.' The suite checks the field writes in both directions; this row is the human check that the dropdown behaves the same way on screen."],
  [203,"Fees & Locks","The office changing somebody's colours charges the same as the portal","1. This is the OFFICE side of the same rule test 102 checks on the customer side. Until 2026-08-21 changing somebody's colours in admin charged nothing, set no window and told nobody - so this is new behaviour and needs a real look.\n2. Pick a TEST customer who already has light colours on file and who is NOT inside a 48-hour window (if you have just added them, wait, or use somebody added a while ago).\n3. Put that test customer on an install route, so the 'already scheduled' half of this gets tested too.\n4. Log into admin (the private office dashboard), click 'Customers' in the menu on the left, then 'All Customers', find them and click 'Edit'.\n5. Change their light colours to something different.\n6. Press Save Changes and READ THE POPUP that appears before anything is saved.\n7. Choose 'Change it and charge $30'.\n8. Open their invoice and look at the total and the lines on it.\n9. Go to Messages and open the System folder.\n10. Now do it a second time on the SAME customer, straight away, and read what the popup says (or whether one appears at all).\n11. Finally, on a different test customer, make a change and this time choose 'Change it without charging'.","At step 6 a popup must appear BEFORE anything is saved, naming the $30, saying whether it goes on their current invoice or next season's bill, and offering three answers: charge, change without charging, and cancel. Cancel must save nothing at all - not the colours, not anything else you changed on the form. After choosing charge, their invoice should show a $30 light-change fee as its OWN line, and the total should go up by exactly $30. In the System folder there should be exactly ONE new note titled 'Lights Changed After Assignment' naming that customer - one, not two. At step 10 the second change should be FREE and should not ask you anything, because they are now inside their 48-hour window - and that window must not be pushed further out by the second change. At step 11 'without charging' must add no fee to the invoice at all, but must STILL hold them off the routes for 48 hours and still raise the note, because that part is about the crew rather than the money.",1,"","","NEW 2026-08-21. The office doing this on the phone is the common case, and the only case now the crew portal is out of use. The automated suite proves the writes happen; what it cannot check is that the popup reads clearly to whoever is on the phone, and that the invoice looks right to a customer who then rings up about it."],
  [103,"Fees & Locks","Fee is enforced server-side","1. This test checks that the $30 fee is charged by Highlighting Utah's computer system (the 'server') itself, not just something the website shows on the screen in your browser — so it can't be tricked or skipped.\n2. Sign in to the Member Portal (the website customers use to manage their own account) as a test customer who is currently outside their free window (meaning a colour change right now would cost $30).\n3. Change their light colours.\n4. When the warning about the $30 fee appears, confirm it.\n5. Log into admin (the private office dashboard) and check that the $30 fee landed on that customer's invoice.\n6. If you have access to someone technical who can try making the same colour change while skipping or bypassing the warning message in the browser, have them try that.","The $30 fee should be added to the invoice no matter what — whether the warning was confirmed normally, or whether someone tried to skip the warning in the browser. This proves the fee is applied by Highlighting Utah's server (its central computer system), not by anything happening in the customer's browser, so it can't be avoided by tricking the browser. It should appear on the invoice as its own separate line, NOT folded into the install price, and it is separate from the $30 new-member fee - a new customer who changes their colours more than 48 hours after joining should end up paying BOTH, $60 in total.",2,"","","RETEST THIS ONE PROPERLY - it was not actually true. Between 2026-08-19 and 2026-08-21 the portal set a flag on a record that had already been saved, so the fee never reached the database at all: every colour change made in the portal charged NOTHING while the portal told the customer in red that $30 had been added. Fixed 2026-08-21. This row is the human check that a real change on a real invoice really does move the money now."],
  [109,"Fees & Locks","Remove light-change fees","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Invoices'.\n3. Click 'Invoice List'.\n4. Open an invoice that has a light-change fee on it.\n5. Write down the balance (the amount currently owed) shown on that invoice.\n6. Click the button labeled 'Remove light-change fee(s)'.\n7. Look at the balance again.","The change-fee line(s) should be gone from the invoice, and the balance should have dropped by exactly the amount of the fee(s) that were removed — no more, no less.",2,"","","Invoices is now its own item in the left menu instead of a tab inside Dashboard - the click-by-click steps were rewritten, so this needs running against the new path."],
  [111,"PayPal","Capture records the payment","1. Use the same test customer from the previous test ('Order amount is the real balance').\n2. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n3. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Invoices'.\n4. Click 'Invoice List'.\n5. Find that customer's invoice and write down their current 'amount paid'.\n6. Sign in to the Member Portal (the website customers use to manage their own account) as that customer and complete a small real PayPal payment.\n7. Go back to admin and check that customer's invoice again.","The 'amount paid' on the invoice should have gone up by exactly the amount that was paid through PayPal, and the balance (amount still owed) should have dropped to match.",3,"","","Every payment now also writes a line to a payment log. After a card payment, open that customer’s invoice in Invoices → Invoice List and check the new \"Payment history\" section shows the amount, the date, and \"Card (PayPal)\"."],
  [112,"PayPal","Webhook is the safety net","1. Sign in to the Member Portal (the website customers use to manage their own account) as a test customer.\n2. Start a small PayPal payment from the portal.\n3. Complete the payment on PayPal's own website/screen.\n4. As soon as the payment is complete, immediately close the browser tab — do not wait for it to send you back to the portal.\n5. Wait a few minutes.\n6. Log into admin (the private office dashboard) and check that customer's invoice.","The payment should still show up as recorded on the invoice, even though you closed the browser tab and it never sent you back to the portal to confirm.",1,"","",""],
  [113,"PayPal","Tips tracked separately","1. Sign in to the Member Portal (the website customers use to manage their own account) as a test customer.\n2. Make a payment that includes a tip added on top.\n3. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n4. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Invoices'.\n5. Click 'Invoice List'.\n6. Open that customer's invoice.\n7. Also, in the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Dashboard' again, then click 'Financial Overview'.","The tip amount should be recorded separately from the regular service payment money — it should show up on its own, not mixed in with what was paid toward the job. The tip should NOT count toward reducing the balance the customer owed for the actual Christmas-light job.",2,"","","Invoices is now its own item in the left menu instead of a tab inside Dashboard - the click-by-click steps were rewritten, so this needs running against the new path."],
  [114,"Nightly Automation","Runs at 7 PM Mountain","1. Log into the admin dashboard (the private office website you log into — not highlightingutah.com, which is the public site customers see).\n2. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click the words 'Invoices'.\n3. Click the tab labeled 'Nightly Automation'.\n4. Find the switch labeled 'Nightly Invoice Automation' and check that it is switched to the ON position.\n5. Make sure at least one test house (a customer record you're using for testing) is marked 'Done' and has not been billed yet.\n6. Leave the app alone and wait until after 7:00 PM.\n7. After 7:00 PM, go back to the 'Nightly Automation' tab and look at the list labeled 'Last 10 nightly runs'.","That evening, an invoice email should have gone out for the test house, and the run you're checking for should be listed in the 'Last 10 nightly runs' list along with the time it ran.",3,"","","Nightly billing now runs per payer, not per house."],
  [121,"Nightly Automation","Summary text arrives","1. Log into the admin dashboard.\n2. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click 'Invoices'.\n3. Click the tab labeled 'Nightly Automation'.\n4. Find the box labeled 'Text a nightly billing summary to this number' and type your own phone number into it.\n5. Click the 'Save' button.\n6. Click the button labeled 'Send Invoices Now'.\n7. Watch your phone for a text message.","A text message should arrive on your phone with a short one-line summary, like '23 sent, 0 errors'. If this text ever stops arriving on a normal night, it means the nightly run did not fire.",3,"","","Nightly billing now runs per payer, not per house."],
  [122,"Nightly Automation","Stale-run banner","1. This is something you watch for happening on its own — you don't need to trigger it yourself.\n2. Log into the admin dashboard.\n3. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click 'Invoices'.\n4. Click the tab labeled 'Nightly Automation'.\n5. Confirm the switch labeled 'Nightly Invoice Automation' is turned ON.\n6. Look at the list labeled 'Last 10 nightly runs'.\n7. Check the date and time of the newest (most recent) run in that list. If it is more than about a day and a half old, look at the top of that card on the screen.","If the nightly run hasn't happened in over about a day and a half, a red warning banner should appear at the top of the card, telling you that billing is turned on but hasn't actually run. That banner is the backup warning for the whole nightly system being dead.",3,"","","Nightly billing now runs per payer, not per house."],
  [124,"Nightly Automation","Missing EmailJS keys fail loudly","1. Log into the admin dashboard.\n2. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click 'Automation Emails'.\n3. Click the tab labeled 'EmailJS Setup' (the keys still live here - only the nightly run itself moved).\n4. Before changing anything, copy the EmailJS keys (the boxes holding the connection codes for the email-sending service) somewhere safe, like a text file, so you can put them back afterward.\n5. Clear out (delete) the EmailJS keys from their boxes.\n6. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click 'Invoices'.\n7. Click the tab labeled 'Nightly Automation'.\n8. Click the button labeled 'Send Invoices Now'.\n9. Read whatever message appears on the screen after clicking that button.\n10. Go back to Automation Emails, then EmailJS Setup, and immediately put the keys back exactly as you copied them.","You should get a clear error message on the screen saying the invoices could not be sent. The app must not act like everything worked fine when the email keys are missing.",3,"","","Nightly billing now runs per payer, not per house."],
  [133,"Inbox & Templates","Drag between folders","1. Open admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Customer Messages'.\n3. Pick any one message sitting in one folder, and drag it with your mouse into a different folder.\n4. Refresh the whole browser page (reload it completely).\n5. Open the folder the message used to be in, and check whether it's still there.\n6. Open the folder you dragged the message into, and check whether it's there.","The message should now be sitting in the new folder you dragged it into, and it should no longer be in the old folder. After refreshing the page, it should still be in the new folder - the move actually saved, it didn't just change on screen temporarily.",1,"","",""],
  [134,"Inbox & Templates","Email templates and folders","1. Open admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Automation Emails'.\n3. Click the tab labeled 'Templates'.\n4. Create a new folder to hold email templates.\n5. Inside that new folder, create a new email template.\n6. Edit the wording of that template.\n7. Save the template.\n8. Go to a place in admin where templates get used - for example, in the left menu click 'Automation Emails', stay on the 'Templates' tab, and open the 'Send to Selected' flow, where a template gets picked.\n9. Check whether your new test template shows up there and can be selected.\n10. Go back to Automation Emails, then Templates, and delete the test template you created.\n11. Delete the test folder you created.","Creating the folder, creating the template inside it, editing its wording, and saving should all work without errors. The new template should actually appear as a selectable choice wherever templates get used, like the Send to Selected flow. Deleting the test template and the test folder afterward should also work cleanly, with nothing left behind.",3,"","","Template folders changed: folders can now be deleted and renamed, and can be nested inside another folder. Re-run it, and this time also make a subfolder inside your test folder and check the parent folder shows its templates too."],
  [135,"Inbox & Templates","Saved templates and custom codes","1. Open admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Automation Emails'.\n3. Click the tab labeled 'Templates'.\n4. Create a template (or use an existing one) and save it.\n5. Create a custom discount code.\n6. Refresh the whole browser page (reload it completely).\n7. Go to a place in admin where a discount can be applied to a customer or invoice.\n8. Try applying the custom discount code you just created.","Both the saved template and the custom discount code should still exist after refreshing the page - neither one should have disappeared. The discount code should actually work when you try to apply it - it should be accepted and applied, not rejected or missing.",1,"","",""],
  [136,"Health & Data","Health Check runs","AUTOMATED - run-all.js runs the Health Check engine itself, confirms all 14 checks are present, and confirms that clean data reports nothing at all (a checker that always finds something is a checker nobody reads). What is left is the screen: that the report renders and the fix buttons appear only on the unambiguous checks.\n\n1. Open admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Health Check'.\n3. Find the checkbox labeled 'Hide checks that pass' and untick it (uncheck it) so every check shows, not just the failing ones.\n4. Click the button labeled 'Run Health Check'.\n5. Read down through the entire list of checks that appears, top to bottom.","Health Check should finish running without throwing any errors, and it should show what it found for every single check in the list. Running it should not change any of your actual data on its own - only the buttons you press yourself, not Health Check itself, are allowed to change data.",3,"","","Health Check now runs on its own a few seconds after login and every ten minutes, so the count badge beside it in the menu appears without anyone pressing anything. There is also a new check for customers with no map pin, and one for customers with no email address."],
  [137,"Health & Data","Import Center","1. Open admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Import Center'.\n3. Prepare a small Excel file with just a couple of rows of payments in it, so you can check the numbers by hand afterward.\n4. Import that Excel file's payments through Import Center.\n5. Open the folder that the imported payments went into.\n6. Compare each imported amount against the matching amount in your original Excel file.","The imported batch of payments should be sitting in the correct folder, and every single amount should match exactly what was in the Excel file, down to the penny.",2,"","","The Import Center card that just told you to go and ask a chatbot has been replaced with a real tool: it gives every existing customer an opening line in the new payment log, and it has a \"Check First\" that writes nothing."],
  [138,"Health & Data","Member Export","1. Open admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Member Export'.\n3. Run the export.\n4. Open the file that downloads.\n5. Count how many rows of customers are in that file.\n6. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Customers'.\n7. Click 'All Customers' and find the total customer count shown there.\n8. Compare the row count from the exported file against that total customer count.","The exported file should have the columns you'd expect for customer information, and the number of rows in the file should match the total number of customers shown under Customers, then All Customers.",1,"","",""],
  [139,"Health & Data","Full Backup","1. Open admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Site Settings'.\n3. Find the card labeled 'Full Backup'.\n4. Click the button that says 'Download Full Backup'.\n5. Open the file that downloads.\n6. Skim through what's inside the file.","The file should download successfully, and when you open it, it should contain your customers, invoices, quotes, routes, employees, and settings - not just one or two of those categories, but all of them.",1,"","",""],
  [140,"Health & Data","No silent permission errors","RETIRED - fully automated, nothing left for a person. This test asked you to hunt the browser console for permission errors. run-all.js now does it properly and on every commit: it checks that EVERY Firestore collection the code touches has a matching rule in firestore.rules, and that every where+orderBy query has a composite index in firestore.indexes.json. A missing rule is the exact cause of a section silently rendering empty, so this is caught before it ships rather than found by accident. Mark this N/A.\n\n1. Open admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Open your browser's console: press the F12 key (on a Mac, press Cmd+Option+I instead).\n3. In the panel that opens, click the tab labeled 'Console'.\n4. Clear out anything already shown in the console (look for a clear or trash-can button, or right-click inside the console and choose 'Clear console').\n5. Starting at the top of the left-side menu in admin, click every single item in the menu one at a time, working your way down to the bottom.\n6. For any item that has sub-tabs, click into every one of those sub-tabs too.\n7. Keep watching the console the entire time you are clicking through everything.","No red permission-denied errors should appear in the console at any point while clicking through the menu. This matters because if part of the database is missing from the security rules, that section of admin just looks empty on screen instead of showing an obvious error - the console is the only place you'd actually catch it.",2,"","","Rules-and-indexes coverage is now checked automatically on every commit. Mark it N/A."],
  [141,"Employees & Payroll","Add an employee","1. Open admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Responsibilities'.\n3. Click the tab labeled 'Employee Names'.\n4. Add a test person: give them a role, a pay type, and set the admin flag (the setting controlling whether they're an admin) however you want it for this test.\n5. Save the new test person.\n6. Open the Crew Portal (the website crew members log into).\n7. Look at the picker where you choose which employee's name you are.","The test person should appear in the employee roster in admin, and their name should also show up as a choice in the Crew Portal's name picker.",1,"","",""],
  [142,"Employees & Payroll","Role templates","1. Open admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Responsibilities'.\n3. Click the tab labeled 'Role Templates'.\n4. Create a new template and choose a specific set of tabs for it (pick whichever tabs you want to test with).\n5. Click the tab labeled 'Employee Names'.\n6. Apply the template you just created to one test employee.\n7. Apply that same template to a second, different test employee.\n8. Open the Crew Portal (the website crew members log into) and sign in as the first test employee.\n9. Write down which tabs you see for them.\n10. Sign out of the Crew Portal.\n11. Sign back in as the second test employee.","Both test employees should see exactly the same set of tabs in the Crew Portal, and that set of tabs should match what you chose in the Role Template.",1,"","",""],
  [143,"Employees & Payroll","Weekly payroll","1. Make sure a few work shifts have already been clocked in through the Crew Portal (the website crew members log into) - clock some in yourself as a test if needed.\n2. Open admin (the private office dashboard).\n3. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Time Logs'.\n4. Click the tab labeled 'Weekly Payroll'.\n5. Pick a specific week to look at.\n6. Pick one person and add up the hours shown for them that week.\n7. Click the tab labeled 'Time Log History' and check that same person's actual clocked shifts for that week against the hours you just added up.\n8. Find someone in the payroll list who worked both warehouse shifts and crew shifts that week, and check their total pay.","The hours in Weekly Payroll should add up correctly to match the actual clocked shifts in Time Log History. For the person who worked both warehouse and crew shifts, their hours should be split out by the correct pay rate for each kind of work, not just lumped together into one total.",2,"","","Payroll now refuses to export a week whose timecards were not loaded, instead of quietly exporting fewer hours than were worked. Page back several weeks and confirm you get a clear red message rather than an empty table."],
  [144,"Employees & Payroll","Time Log History","1. Open admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Time Logs'.\n3. Click the tab labeled 'Time Log History'.\n4. Use the filters to narrow the list down to just one specific person and one specific week.\n5. Pick a few of the shifts shown and check the date, start time, end time, and length against what that person actually worked.","The dates, times, and shift lengths shown should all be correct, and after applying the filters, the list should genuinely only show that one person's shifts from that one week - not shifts from other people or other weeks mixed in.",2,"","","Time Logs now loads by date (two seasons back) instead of stopping at the most recent 300 shifts. An older week used to look identical to a week nobody worked."],
  [145,"Employees & Payroll","Timecard change request","1. Open the Crew Portal (the website crew members log into) and sign in as a test employee.\n2. Submit a correction request for one of that employee's shifts.\n3. Open admin (the private office dashboard).\n4. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Time Logs'.\n5. Find the change request you just submitted.\n6. Before approving anything, click the tab labeled 'Time Log History' and check what that shift currently shows.\n7. Go back and approve the change request.\n8. Check the same shift in Time Log History again.","The shift should show its original, unchanged values right up until you approve the request. Only after you approve it should the shift's values actually change to match the correction.",1,"","",""],
  [146,"Employees & Payroll","Crews config","1. Open admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Responsibilities'.\n3. Click the tab labeled 'Crews'.\n4. Set how many crews there should be.\n5. Assign people to each crew.\n6. Save your changes.\n7. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Routes'.\n8. Click 'Generate Route'.\n9. Click 'Install'.\n10. Open the dropdown menu used for picking a crew.","The crews and the people you just set up in Responsibilities should be exactly the ones listed when you open the crew dropdown while generating an install route.",1,"","",""],
  [147,"Employees & Payroll","Crew overrides","1. Open admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Responsibilities'.\n3. Click the tab labeled 'One-Day Reassignment'.\n4. Move one person to a different crew, but only for a single specific day.\n5. Check the crew list for that specific day to confirm the move took effect.\n6. Check the crew list for the day before that day.\n7. Check the crew list for the day after that day.","Only the one specific day you changed should show the person on their new crew. The day before and the day after should both still show that person on their normal, regular crew - unaffected by the one-day move.",1,"","",""],
  [148,"Employees & Payroll","Employee categories","1. Open admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Responsibilities'.\n3. Click the tab labeled 'Employee Names'.\n4. Create a new category.\n5. Tag one employee with that new category.\n6. Tag a second, different employee with that same category.\n7. Refresh the whole browser page (reload it completely).","The category and the tags on both employees should still be there after refreshing the page - nothing should disappear. You should be able to use that category afterward to group or filter the two tagged employees together.",1,"","",""],
  [149,"Finance","Expenses","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard - the private website you use to run the business, not highlightingutah.com, which is the public site customers see).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word 'Dashboard'.\n3. Click 'Financial Overview'.\n4. Click the tab labeled 'Manage & Expenses'.\n5. Look at the total shown on the screen right now, and write it down - this is the total before you make any changes.\n6. Add a new expense (pick any amount) and save it.\n7. Look at the total again and write it down.\n8. Click on the expense you just added so you can edit it.\n9. Change the amount to a different number and save it.\n10. Look at the total again and write it down.\n11. Delete the expense you added.\n12. Look at the total one final time and write it down.","Every time you added, edited, or deleted the expense, the total on the dashboard changed to match. Adding it made the total go up by that amount, editing it changed the total by the right amount, and deleting it brought the total back down to what it was before you started.",1,"","",""],
  [150,"Finance","Bills","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word 'Dashboard'.\n3. Click 'Financial Overview'.\n4. Click the tab labeled 'Debt & Bills'.\n5. Add a recurring bill: type in an amount and pick a due date, then save it.\n6. Click the tab labeled 'Budget & Goals'.","The bill you just added shows up in the Debt & Bills list, and it is also counted in the numbers on the Budget & Goals tab - it should not be missing or ignored there.",1,"","",""],
  [151,"Finance","Budget and savings goals","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word 'Dashboard'.\n3. Click 'Financial Overview'.\n4. Click the tab labeled 'Budget & Goals'.\n5. Create a budget category: give it a name, type in a monthly amount, and save it.\n6. Create a savings goal: give it a name, type in a target amount, and save it.\n7. Refresh the page (press F5, or click your browser's refresh button).","After refreshing, both the budget category and the savings goal are still there - they were saved, not lost. Both should show some kind of progress indicator (a number, bar, or percentage) showing how far along they are toward their monthly amount or savings target.",1,"","",""],
  [152,"Finance","Debt accounts","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word 'Dashboard'.\n3. Click 'Financial Overview'.\n4. Click the tab labeled 'Debt & Bills'.\n5. Add a debt account: give it a name, type in a balance amount, and save it.\n6. Refresh the page (press F5, or click your browser's refresh button).","After refreshing, the debt account is still listed, and the balance shown next to it matches the exact amount you typed in.",2,"","","Total revenue and this month’s income now include tips. They were charged to cards and counted in no figure at all, so the Dashboard understated what actually came in."],
  [153,"Finance","Credit cards and transactions","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word 'Dashboard'.\n3. Click the tab labeled 'Business Credit Cards'.\n4. Add a card: give it a name and fill in whatever other details it asks for, then save it.\n5. On that card, log a transaction: type in an amount and save it.\n6. Write down the exact amount you typed for that transaction.\n7. Look at the balance shown for that card.","The transaction you logged appears in the list for that card. The card's balance changed by exactly the amount of the transaction you logged - not more, not less.",1,"","",""],
  [154,"Finance","Card payments and recurring overrides","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word 'Dashboard'.\n3. Click the tab labeled 'Business Credit Cards'.\n4. Pick any card that already has a balance greater than zero.\n5. Record a payment against that card: type in a payment amount and save it.\n6. Set a recurring override for one specific month - this changes the normal recurring amount just for that one month.\n7. Look at the card's balance.\n8. Look at the month you set the override for.\n9. Look at the month right before it, and the month right after it.","The card's balance went down by the exact amount of the payment you recorded. The override you set only shows up on the one specific month you chose - the month before it and the month after it are unaffected and show the normal, non-override amount.",1,"","",""],
  [155,"Finance","Yearly snapshot","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word 'Dashboard'.\n3. Click 'Financial Overview'.\n4. Find and click the option to take a year-end snapshot - this saves a copy of the current numbers.\n5. Refresh the page (press F5, or click your browser's refresh button).\n6. Open that same snapshot again.","The snapshot you took is still there after refreshing, and when you open it again, the figures inside it are exactly the same as when you first took it - nothing has changed or drifted.",2,"","","The Snapshots list has a new \"Payment records\" column. A snapshot taken by Start New Season carries the per-customer payment record for that season and can be downloaded as a spreadsheet — that file is what answers \"I paid you in November\". Note the delete button beside it now throws away the only remaining copy."],
  [156,"Site Content","Reviews","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard - the private website you use to run the business, not highlightingutah.com, which is the public site customers see).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word 'Reviews'.\n3. Add a new review - fill in whatever it asks for (such as a name and review text) - and save it.\n4. Open a new browser tab and go to highlightingutah.com.\n5. Find the reviews section on that page and check whether your new review is there.\n6. Go back to the admin browser tab.\n7. Edit the review you just added - change something in it - and save it.\n8. Go back to the highlightingutah.com browser tab, refresh the page, and check the reviews section again.\n9. Go back to the admin browser tab and delete the review you added.\n10. Go back to the highlightingutah.com browser tab, refresh the page, and check the reviews section one more time.","Every time - after adding, after editing, and after deleting - the reviews section on highlightingutah.com matched exactly what was in admin. When you added the review it appeared on the public site, when you edited it the edit showed up on the public site, and when you deleted it, it disappeared from the public site.",1,"","",""],
  [157,"Site Content","Gallery","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word 'Gallery'.\n3. Add a photo - upload one and save it.\n4. Open a new browser tab and go to highlightingutah.com (the public site customers see).\n5. Find the gallery section and check whether your new photo is there.\n6. Go back to the admin browser tab and remove the photo you just added.\n7. Go back to the highlightingutah.com browser tab, refresh the page, and check the gallery again.","Both times you checked, the public gallery on highlightingutah.com matched what admin had. After you added the photo, it showed up on highlightingutah.com. After you removed it, the photo disappeared from highlightingutah.com.",1,"","",""],
  [158,"Site Content","Hero images","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word 'Hero Images' (these are the large photos shown at the very top of the public homepage).\n3. Either change which images are being used, or change the order they appear in, and save your changes.\n4. Open a new browser tab and go to highlightingutah.com (the public site customers see).\n5. Look at the very top of the homepage.","The images at the top of the highlightingutah.com homepage are exactly the ones you set in admin, and they appear in the same order you put them in.",1,"","",""],
  [159,"Site Content","FAQ","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word 'FAQ'.\n3. Add a new question and its answer, then save it.\n4. Open a new browser tab and go to highlightingutah.com (the public site customers see).\n5. Find the FAQ section and check whether your new question and answer are there.\n6. Go back to the admin browser tab and edit the question or answer you just added, then save it.\n7. Go back to the highlightingutah.com browser tab, refresh the page, and check the FAQ section again.\n8. Go back to the admin browser tab and delete the question you added.\n9. Go back to the highlightingutah.com browser tab, refresh the page, and check the FAQ section one more time.","After every single change - adding, editing, and deleting - the FAQ section on highlightingutah.com matched exactly what was in admin.",1,"","",""],
  [160,"Site Content","Site settings","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word 'Site Settings'.\n3. Change the hero text (the main headline text shown on the homepage).\n4. Change the contact details (such as phone number or email).\n5. Save your changes.\n6. Add a new city to the service-area list (the list of cities the business serves), and save that too.\n7. Open a new browser tab and go to highlightingutah.com (the public site customers see).\n8. Read the homepage and check that your new hero text is showing there.\n9. Check that the contact details shown on the site match what you typed.\n10. Near the top of the page, find and click the gold button that says 'Free Quote'.\n11. In the form that pops up, click the dropdown for city.","The hero text and contact details you typed into admin are now showing on highlightingutah.com. The new city you added appears as an option in the city dropdown on the Free Quote form.",1,"","",""],
  [161,"Security","Portal hides internal figures","1. Go to the Member Portal (the part of highlightingutah.com that a real customer logs into to see their own account) and sign in using a real customer's information.\n2. Click the 'Payment' tab and read everything on it carefully.\n3. Click the 'Information' tab and read everything on it carefully.\n4. Click the 'Light Colors' tab and read everything on it carefully.\n5. Click the 'Changes' tab and read everything on it carefully.\n6. Click the 'Contact' tab and read everything on it carefully.\n7. Click the 'Cancel' tab and read everything on it carefully.\n8. On every tab, look closely for any of these four things that a customer should NOT be able to see: their customer number, their bin count, their house difficulty rating, or your per-foot pricing (how much you charge per foot of lights).","None of those four things - customer number, bin count, house difficulty rating, or per-foot pricing - appear anywhere in the Member Portal, on any of the tabs. The office's private business information stays private; the customer only sees their own information, because the server strips those fields out before the page ever gets them.",2,"","","A quote link could be traded for a customer's portal access, home address and gate code. Confirm a quote link shows quote details only."],
  [162,"Security","Portal writes only through functions","1. This test needs someone comfortable using a web browser's developer console (a technical, built-in browser tool) - ask that person to run it.\n2. Go to the Member Portal and sign in using a real customer's phone number and last name.\n3. Using the browser's developer console, attempt to write (save) a change directly to the database, instead of using one of the portal's own Save buttons.","The direct write attempt is refused and blocked. The database will not accept changes unless they go through the portal's normal Save buttons, which go through the server. This confirms a customer cannot use the browser console to edit things they aren't allowed to edit.",1,"","",""],
  [163,"Security","Rate limiting","1. Go to the Member Portal.\n2. On purpose, type in a phone number and last name that you know are wrong - not a real customer's information.\n3. Click or press the 'Sign In' button.\n4. Repeat steps 2 and 3 quickly, ten to fifteen times in a row, without waiting between attempts.","After several wrong attempts in a row, the portal starts slowing you down - for example, making you wait longer between tries - or it blocks you from trying again, instead of letting you keep guessing forever.",1,"","",""],
  [165,"Security","Shared staff login is app-side only","1. This test does not require you to click through anything in the app - it is something to read and confirm you understand.\n2. Read and note this fact: the whole crew shares one single staff login, and then each person picks their own name after logging in.\n3. Understand what that means: any limits on which tabs a particular crew member can see are enforced by the phone app itself (the software), not by the database that stores the actual data.","Confirm that you understand this known limitation: anyone who has the shared staff login technically has full database-level access to everything, no matter which tabs the app shows or hides for them. Mark this test 'Pass' once you have read this and accept it as-is, or mark it 'Fail' if you think this should be changed.",1,"","",""],
  [167,"Admin","Quote photo upload","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \"Quote Requests\".\n3. Find any quote card.\n4. On that card, click the button called \"Add Photo\" (or \"Add More Photos\" if a photo is already there).\n5. Upload a house photo directly on the card.\n6. Click the button called \"Mark Up Photo\".\n7. Try drawing on the photo.","The photo you uploaded should appear on the quote card. Clicking Mark Up Photo should open a drawing tool that lets you draw on the photo. This is the manual photo-upload feature that replaced an old Street View lookup tool — it's a live feature that's actually used, not leftover code, so it should keep working correctly.",2,"","",""],
  [168,"Admin","Bulk Difficulty column","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Customers'.\n3. Click the tab called 'Bulk Updates'.\n4. Click the heading 'Customers & Routes' to open it if it isn't already open.\n5. Scroll down to the row of boxes that has 'Wire Color', 'Outlet Timer' and 'Use Eaves'. There should now be a fourth box there called 'Difficulty (optional)'.\n6. Pick two real customers who are currently Unrated. Write down their street addresses exactly as they appear in Routes.\n7. Paste those two street addresses into the 'Street Address' box, one per line.\n8. In the 'Difficulty' box, type Hard on the first line and easy (lowercase, on purpose) on the second line.\n9. Check that the small grey counter above the Difficulty box says '2 rows'.\n10. Click 'Import / Update Customers' and wait for it to finish.\n11. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Routes'.\n12. Search for each of those two customers and click 'Show Details' on their card.\n13. Now go back to Bulk Updates and try it again with the same two addresses, but this time type the word Banana in the Difficulty box on the first line.\n14. Click 'Import / Update Customers' again.","After step 10, the first customer's difficulty should read Hard and the second should read Easy — typing it in lowercase should still work. Leaving the Difficulty box completely empty should never change anyone's existing difficulty. In step 14, the import should refuse to run and show a message naming row 1 and the word Banana, and nothing at all should be imported or changed from that attempt.",1,"","",""],
  [170,"Invoices","Printed invoice shows a number and a due date","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Automation Emails'.\n3. Click the tab labeled 'Invoices'.\n4. Tick one customer who still owes money, to show their invoice.\n5. Read the top right corner of the invoice that appears, and then the top left.","The top right should show an invoice number starting 'HU-', the season year, an 'Issued' date, and a 'Due' date exactly 30 days after the issued date. The business phone, email and website should appear under the Highlighting Utah name on the left.",1,"","",""],
  [171,"Invoices","Printed invoice has no internal details","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Automation Emails'.\n3. Click the tab labeled 'Invoices'.\n4. Tick any one customer to show their invoice.\n5. Read the whole invoice from top to bottom.","There should be no customer number anywhere on it, no 'Status: Not yet installed' line, and no mention of takedown or removal. The old 'Details' block should be gone entirely and Bill-to should run the full width. It should also no longer claim it is what the nightly email sends, because that was never true.",1,"","",""],
  [172,"Invoices","Printed invoice: a credit is shown once","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Pick a test customer who has a credit on their invoice, or add one to a test invoice.\n3. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Automation Emails'.\n4. Click the tab labeled 'Invoices'.\n5. Tick that customer to show their invoice.\n6. Find the credit and count how many times it appears.\n7. Add up the line items and compare the answer against the Total.","The credit should appear exactly once, as its own line in the table with its reason. There should be no separate 'Credits' row down in the totals. The line items should add up to the Total, and the Total less anything already paid should equal the Amount due.",1,"","",""],
  [173,"Invoices","Printed invoice: multi-house shows every property","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Find a payer billed for more than one house (several houses billed to the same phone number).\n3. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Automation Emails'.\n4. Click the tab labeled 'Invoices'.\n5. Tick that payer to show their invoice.\n6. Read the line items and the top right corner.","Each house should have its own row showing its street address, its own footage and rate underneath, and its own price on the right. The top right should say how many properties the invoice covers. It should NOT show one single footage line taken from the payer's own house, which is what made these impossible to check before.",2,"","","Per-property rows are now editable from the invoice panel."],
  [174,"Invoices","Printed invoice: paid invoices look different","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Pick a test customer whose invoice is paid in full.\n3. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Automation Emails'.\n4. Click the tab labeled 'Invoices'.\n5. Tick that customer to show their invoice.\n6. Compare it against an unpaid customer's invoice.","On the paid one the 'How to pay' block should be gone, the due date replaced by the date they paid, the payment status box green, and the bottom line reading 'Amount paid' rather than 'Amount due'. The unpaid one should still show How to pay with the portal address, the Venmo handle and cheque wording.",1,"","",""],
  [179,"Invoices","Billing emails carry a Pay button","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click 'Automation Emails'.\n3. Click the tab labeled 'Templates', then open the 'Billing' folder.\n4. Open the template called 'Nightly Auto-Invoice - Unpaid' and check it reads 'Pay your invoice here:' followed by the pay_button token rather than a bare portal_link.\n5. Open the template called 'Nightly Auto-Invoice - Paid Receipt' and check it uses the view_portal_button token.\n6. Send yourself a test of each and open both emails.","The unpaid email should show a gold button reading 'Pay Your Invoice' with a short line of text introducing it. The receipt should show a button reading 'View Your Portal' and no 'pay your invoice here' line, since they owe nothing. Both buttons should open that customer's own portal without them having to log in.",1,"","",""],
  [180,"Invoices","Paying in full sends a receipt","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Pick a test customer who has your own email address on their record and still owes money.\n3. Click 'Invoices' in the left menu, then 'Invoice List', and find them.\n4. Change their payment status to 'Paid in Full'.\n5. Check the inbox for that email address.","A receipt should arrive using the 'Nightly Auto-Invoice - Paid Receipt' wording, showing the amount paid and a 'View Your Portal' button. Admin should also briefly show a 'Receipt emailed' message.",1,"","",""],
  [181,"Invoices","Paying part sends the balance email","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Pick a test customer with your own email on their record who owes, say, $500.\n3. Click 'Invoices', then 'Invoice List', and find them.\n4. Change their payment status to Partial Payment and enter $200 when asked how much they have paid.\n5. Check that inbox.","An email should arrive using the new 'Payment Received - Balance Remaining' template: it should confirm the $200 received, show the invoice total, the amount paid so far, and $300 still due, with 'Pay Your Invoice' and 'Pay with Venmo' buttons. It should NOT say they are paid in full.",1,"","",""],
  [182,"Invoices","Small payments and corrections stay quiet","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. On a test customer with your own email, record a payment of $5.\n3. Check the inbox - nothing should arrive.\n4. Now record a payment of $200 and let the receipt arrive.\n5. Change the amount paid back down to $150, as if fixing a typo.\n6. Check the inbox again.\n7. Finally, set the same amount again without changing it.","The $5 payment should send nothing, because it is under the $10 minimum. Correcting the figure downward should send nothing. Re-saving the same figure should send nothing. Only the genuine $200 payment should have produced an email.",2,"","","Corrections are now RECORDED rather than hidden. If you lower a customer’s paid figure, the payment history should show it as a negative \"Correction\" line — that is deliberate, because a balance going down is exactly what customers ring up about."],
  [183,"Invoices","A receipt is never sent twice for one payment","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. On a test customer with your own email, record a payment that clears their balance.\n3. Wait for the receipt to arrive.\n4. Without changing the amount, set the payment status to Paid in Full again.\n5. Check the inbox.\n6. If you can, also pay a test invoice through PayPal in the Member Portal and watch for duplicates.","Only one receipt should ever arrive for the same amount paid. The invoice records what figure it last sent a receipt for, which is also what stops a PayPal payment and its background confirmation both emailing the same customer.",1,"","",""],
  [184,"Invoices","Importing payments asks before emailing","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Click 'Invoices', then the 'Import / Export' tab.\n3. Import a small test payment file with two or three rows.\n4. Read the question that appears before the import runs.\n5. Choose Cancel on that question the first time, and check nobody was emailed.\n6. Import again and choose OK this time.","Before importing it should ask whether to email receipts, and say how many rows the file has so you know how much mail could go out. Cancelling should still import the payments but send nothing. Choosing OK should import and then report how many receipts were emailed.",1,"","",""],
  [185,"Invoices","PayPal payments receipt themselves","1. Open the Member Portal as a test customer who owes money and has your own email on their record.\n2. Pay through PayPal.\n3. Check that inbox.\n4. Check the invoice in admin.","A receipt should arrive without anyone touching admin, because PayPal payments are receipted by the server rather than the browser. If the payment cleared the balance it should be the Paid Receipt wording; if it only covered part, the Balance Remaining one. The invoice should show the payment recorded.",2,"","","PayPal payments are logged once, not twice, even though both the browser and the PayPal webhook report the same payment. Check a card payment produces exactly ONE line in the payment history."],
  [186,"Invoices","Send one invoice to yourself","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left, click 'Automation Emails', then the 'Invoices' tab.\n3. Find the card headed 'Send yourself one customer's invoice'.\n4. Pick a customer who still owes money, put your own email address in the box, and press 'Send me this invoice'.\n5. Open your inbox.\n6. Then check that customer in the Invoice List, and ask them if they got anything.","You should receive that customer's real invoice, with a note at the top saying it is a test copy. The figures should match what their invoice actually says. The customer themselves should receive nothing, their invoice should be unchanged, no $30 fee should have appeared, and they should not be marked as billed.",1,"","",""],
  [187,"Invoices","Test send picks the right template","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Go to 'Automation Emails', then the 'Invoices' tab.\n3. Send yourself the invoice of a customer who still owes money.\n4. Now send yourself the invoice of a customer who is paid in full.\n5. Compare the two emails, and read the confirmation line under the button each time.","The unpaid customer's email should use the 'Nightly Auto-Invoice - Unpaid' wording with an amount due and a Pay button. The paid one should use the Paid Receipt wording. The line under the button should name which template it used and the amount, so you can tell at a glance which one went.",1,"","",""],
  [195,"Warehouse","One request per member, topped up not duplicated","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word \"Warehouse\", then the tab called \"Build\".\n3. In \"Add From Inbox\", start typing a test customer's name in the first box and pick them from the list.\n4. Look at the Pattern box and the \"How many bundles\" box without typing anything into them.\n5. Click \"Add to Queue\".\n6. Now do steps 3 to 5 again for the SAME customer.\n7. Read the pop-up that appears, and press OK.\n8. Look at the queue below for that customer.\n9. Now pick that same customer again, change \"What is this for?\" to \"Timer\", set it to 1, and click \"Add to Queue\". If a warning about their record appears, read it and press OK.\n10. Look at the queue below for that customer again.\n11. Finally, pick that customer, leave it on Lights, but type a different colour into the Pattern box and click \"Add to Queue\".","At step 4 the pattern should already be filled in from their record and the bundles should already be worked out from their measured feet - you should not have to type either. At step 7 the pop-up should say they already have a request and offer to make it a bigger total. At step 8 there should be ONE heading with that customer's name, not two. At step 10 the timer should be a second line under that SAME heading, with the total shown in the heading. At step 11 you should get an \"are you sure\" pop-up first, because what you typed no longer matches their record.",1,"","",""],
  [197,"Warehouse","Print the build sheet as a spreadsheet","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word \"Warehouse\".\n3. Click the tab called \"Build\" and read what is on screen - which colour groups there are, and roughly how many houses are in each.\n4. Press the button called \"Print Build Sheet\" (it is just above the list).\n5. A new tab should open with the sheet in it and the print box should come up on its own. If nothing happens at all, your browser has blocked the pop-up - allow pop-ups for this site and press the button again.\n6. Instead of printing straight away, look at the preview: check the column headings across the top, and check the groups and the houses are the same ones, in the same order, as the tab you were just looking at.\n7. Pick one house you can see on both, and check its bundle number on paper matches the bundle number on screen.\n8. Print it, or save it as a PDF.\n9. Optional, if you want it in Excel: select the whole table in that tab, copy it, and paste it into a blank Excel or Google Sheets file.\n10. Go back to admin, click the tab called \"Recycle\", and press \"Print Recycle Sheet\" there.","The sheet must be a plain grid - one row per house or item, with headings across the top (Group, Wire, Type, Customer / Item, Address, Bin #, Feet, Bundles, Timer, Notes) and an empty tick box at the start of every row. It must list the same groups, in the same order, as the Build tab, with the group name repeated on every row rather than only in a heading. A house with no measured feet on file should leave the Feet box empty rather than printing a 0. A house that wants a timer should say YES in the Timer column. If it runs to a second page, the column headings should repeat at the top of that page. Pasting it into Excel should land in proper columns, not all in one cell. The Recycle sheet should do the same for the recycle list, with a column for the bin number that is coming back into the pool. With nothing to build, the sheet should say so rather than printing an empty grid.",1,"","","New button, 2026-08-14. The Warehouse tab is still grouped and collapsible on screen, which does not work on paper - you cannot tick a card off, and it will not open in a spreadsheet. The printed version is the same list flattened to one row per line. It is built from the same grouping code the screen uses, so the two cannot drift apart, but that is exactly what is worth checking by eye the first time."],
  [194,"Warehouse","What each house needs, on the build row","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word \"Customers\", then \"All Customers\".\n3. Click Edit on a test customer who is in the build queue. Set \"Outlet Timer\" to \"Yes\", set a wire colour, type something into their notes, and click Save.\n4. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word \"Warehouse\", then the tab called \"Build\".\n5. Press the \"Show houses\" button on that customer's colour group.\n6. Look at that customer's row.\n7. Look at the very top of the Build tab for a group called \"Timers\" and press its \"Show houses\" button.\n8. Now find a brand-new customer in the build list who has not been given a customer number yet, and look at their row.","That customer's row should show a \"Timer\" tag, their wire colour, their customer number, and their notes underneath. The \"Timers\" group at the top should list that customer by name and say how many houses in this build need a timer. The brand-new customer with no number should show an orange warning saying their number still needs assigning, instead of just showing nothing.",1,"","",""],
  [193,"Warehouse","Rejoining after being recycled","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word \"Customers\", then \"All Customers\".\n3. Click Edit on a test customer, set \"RSVP\" to \"No\", and click Save.\n4. Go to Warehouse, click the tab called \"Recycle\", find that customer and mark them as recycled. Write down the customer number that comes off them.\n5. Now go back to Customers, click Edit on that same customer, set \"RSVP\" to \"Yes\", and click Save.\n6. Click the tab called \"Build\" in Warehouse and look for that customer.\n7. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \"Inbox\" and look in the \"System\" folder.\n8. Repeat the whole thing a second time, but at step 5 use the Member Portal as that customer and press Yes there instead of using admin - the answer should be identical either way.","After step 5 that customer should be in the Warehouse Build list, because their old lights were taken apart and a new bundle has to be made. There should be a note in the Inbox System folder saying they rejoined after recycling, that their lights need rebuilding and that they need a customer number assigned. Their record should still show NO customer number - the system must not hand one out by itself, because you may have already written a number on a bin by hand. Doing it through the Member Portal instead of admin should give exactly the same result.",1,"","",""],
  [199,"Routes","Print the crew's day as a spreadsheet","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word \"Schedule\".\n3. On the day list down the left, click the day the crew is working - normally today.\n4. Read what is on screen: how many stops that day has, and the first two or three names in order.\n5. At the top right of that day's panel, press the button called \"Print This Day\".\n6. A new tab should open with the sheet in it and the print box should come up on its own. If nothing happens at all, your browser has blocked the pop-up - allow pop-ups for this site and press the button again.\n7. Look at the preview before printing: check the column headings across the top, and check the stops are the same ones in the same order as the panel, numbered 1, 2, 3 down the page.\n8. Print it, or save it as a PDF.\n9. Click the \"Fixes\" tab at the top of the Schedule dashboard, pick a fixer route on the left, and press \"Print This Day\" there too.\n10. Click the \"Takedowns\" tab, pick a takedown day, and press \"Print This Day\" there as well.\n11. Go back to the \"Scheduling\" tab and press \"Print Whole Plan\", next to the Export CSV button. Scroll past the table to the bottom of that sheet: any day that has a new hang on it should have a block of house photos there, under a heading naming that day.\n12. Optional, if you want it in Excel: select the whole table in that tab, copy it, and paste it into a blank Excel or Google Sheets file.","Each sheet must be a plain grid - one row per stop, with headings across the top (Stop, CU #, Name, Address, City, Phone, Type, Price, Details, Already done) and an empty tick box at the start of every row, so the crew can tick houses off as they go. The stops must be in the same order as the panel on screen, numbered from 1. A house with no price on the plan should leave the Price box empty rather than printing $0, and a house with no phone number should leave that box empty too. The Type column should say INSTALL, NEW or FIX so the crew can tell them apart, and anything already ticked off in the app should say \"done\". \"Print This Day\" prints the WHOLE day - both crews together. It must work the same way on all three tabs: Scheduling, Fixes and Takedowns. \"Print Whole Plan\" should print every day, with the date and the route repeated on every row so it can be sorted in a spreadsheet - it must stay ONE table, not one table per day, or it can no longer be sorted. After that table it should carry the new hangs' house photos, in a block per day, each block headed with that day's date. Only new hangs: a house the crew has hung before must NOT have a picture on it. A day with no new hangs should have no block and no heading at all rather than an empty one, and a new hang with no photo on file should simply be left out rather than printing an empty frame. The pictures should be the same size as on the crew sheets - about an inch and a half tall, lying the long way with the paper - with the name and number underneath the picture, never across it. If it runs to more than one page, the column headings should repeat at the top of each page. Pasting it into Excel should land in proper columns, not all in one cell.",3,"","","Print Whole Plan now carries the new hangs' house photos, which it was the last printer without - the crew sheet, the whole-day sheet and the per-day button all had them already. The automated suite proves the right houses get a picture and the wrong ones do not; what it cannot check is how it comes out on paper, which is the whole reason this row exists. Look at the printed sheet, not the screen: the size of the pictures, that no caption sits across a garage door, and that the table above them can still be sorted in Excel."],
  [200,"Routes","Two crews, a city each, and their own sheets","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word \"Schedule\".\n3. Just under the row of tabs at the top, find the box headed \"Crews\".\n4. There should be two crews. Type a real name over \"Crew 1\" - for example the names of the two people on it - then click somewhere else on the page so it saves.\n5. Do the same for \"Crew 2\".\n6. Refresh the whole browser page, come back to Schedule, and check both names are still there.\n7. Look at the grey text next to each crew: it should name the city that crew has, and how many stops they have on the day showing.\n8. Leave the city boxes on \"Auto\" for now. Click a different day on the left and watch whether the cities update to that day.\n9. Now set Crew 1's city box to a real town, and Crew 2's to a different town.\n10. On the day panel on the right, press the button with the FIRST crew's name on it.\n11. Look at the sheet that opens, then close it and press the button with the SECOND crew's name.\n12. If a yellow warning appears in the Crews box saying some stops are in neither city, press \"Print This Day\" as well and check those stops are on that sheet.","There should be exactly two crews, and both names should save when you click away and still be there after a refresh - they are stored with the route plan, so they are the same on any device you open admin on. On \"Auto\", the two crews should pick two DIFFERENT cities from whatever day you are looking at (the busiest one and the next busiest), and those should change as you click through days. Setting a city by hand should override Auto and stay put on every day. Each crew's print button must open a sheet with THAT CREW'S NAME as the heading at the top, listing only the houses in that crew's city, numbered 1, 2, 3 for that crew - not the whole day filtered down with gaps in the numbering. A crew with nothing to do that day should have no button at all rather than printing an empty page. Nothing may go missing: if some stops are in neither crew's city, the Crews box must say so in yellow, and those stops must still appear on the \"Print This Day\" sheet.",1,"","","New feature, 2026-08-14. Two crews, each working one city, each printing its own sheet with its own name at the top. The names are yours to set. The city is on Auto by default because the plan does not cover the same two towns every day - pin a city to a crew if your season really does work the same two towns throughout."],
  [201,"Admin","RSVP email says who they are paying for","1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left, click \"Customers\", then the tab called \"Who Pays for Whom\". Pick any payer on that list who is covering more than one house and note their name and the names under them.\n3. Now click \"Automation\" in the left menu, then the \"Automation Emails\" tab, and find the RSVP email section.\n4. In the preview picker, choose that same payer. Read the preview to the bottom.\n5. Send the RSVP email to yourself: put your own email address on a test customer, or send to that payer only if you are happy for them to receive it.\n6. Open the email that arrives, on a phone as well as a computer if you can.","The preview and the real email both end with a shaded box headed \"Who you are paying for this year\", listing every house on that person\u2019s bill by NAME with its address and price, and a total underneath. The names must be exactly the ones the Who Pays for Whom tab showed, and anyone who said No for this season must NOT be listed. A customer who only pays for their own house gets no box at all \u2014 check one of those too. The box must be readable on a phone.",1,"","","New feature. A payer covering several houses was being asked to commit for the season without being told which houses that covered. Only a real send can prove the box survives the email software and looks right on a phone \u2014 everything else about this is covered by the automated tests."],
  [202,"Member Portal","An existing member is not sent the new-customer form","1. Open the admin site and click \"Quote Requests\", then the \"Closed\" tab.\n2. Find a quote that has already been made into a customer - it shows a green \"Converted\" mark. Note whose it is.\n3. Open that same customer's quote email (or copy the link off the card) and press the big gold \"View Your Quote\" button, as if you were them.\n4. If you are asked which answer you meant, press \"Approve my quote\".\n5. Read what comes up, then press \"No, keep everything the same\".\n6. Go back and open the same link one more time, and this time press \"Yes, I'd like to make a change\".","You should NOT see the install details form (light colours, wire, timer) at any point - they are already a customer and we already hold all of that. Instead you should be asked \"Do you want anything changed with your lights this year?\" with two buttons. \"No\" should leave a warm message saying we can't wait to light up their home again this year. \"Yes\" should take them to the Member Portal - either straight into their account if that phone has signed in before, or to the sign-in box with their email or phone already filled in and only the last name left to type. It must never sign them in without the last name.",1,"","","New, 2026-08-19. The automated suite already proves the question renders, both buttons work, and the quote link is never turned into a login. What it cannot prove is that your REAL converted quotes actually carry the converted mark - so this is checking real records, not the mechanism. If you see the install details form instead of the question, that quote's card never got closed when the customer was created; close it by hand and it will behave."],
  [207, "Admin", "Measure Roof — sky view and Street View stay in step", "1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left, click \"Quote Requests\".\n3. On any quote card, click the button that says \"Measure Roof\". A window opens with that customer’s address already filled in.\n4. Wait a moment. The top picture should be the satellite view looking straight down at that house, and the picture under it should be Street View looking at the front of it.\n5. Click \"Start Measuring\", then click along the edge of the roof on the SATELLITE picture — four or five clicks. While you are doing it, try to drag the map: it should refuse to move.\n6. Now look at the Street View picture underneath. The line you just drew should be showing on it too.\n7. Drag one of the dots you placed. Both pictures should follow it.\n8. Click \"Set the Wall\", then click where the wall meets the ground at each end of the house. Click \"Start Measuring\" again and this time click along the roof edge on the STREET VIEW picture — that line should appear on the satellite picture too.\n9. Click \"Capture Sky View\", drag a box over the house to crop it, and click \"Add to Quote List\". Do the same with \"Capture Street View\".\n10. Click the \"Attach 2 to Quote\" button at the bottom, then look at the quote card behind.", "The house comes up in both pictures from the address alone. A line drawn in either picture appears in BOTH of them, and dragging a point moves it in both — they are two windows onto one set of points, not two drawings. Neither picture moves while you are drawing. The footage adds up under Distance Results, and the figure offered for Estimated Feet is DOUBLE the measured one and says so on screen. Both captured pictures end up on the quote card as photos, and nothing was ever downloaded to the computer.\n\nIf the two Capture buttons say Google will not hand over the picture, that is the one thing this cannot fix by itself: the Google key needs \"Maps Static API\" and \"Street View Static API\" added to its API restrictions list in Google Cloud Console (APIs & Services → Credentials → the key → API restrictions). Measuring itself should still work while that is outstanding — only the picture is blocked.", 1, "", "", ""],,
  [208,"Admin","Declining a quote takes the customer out of the season","\u26a0 USE A TEST CUSTOMER. This test deliberately takes somebody out of the season and flags their lights for recycling.\n1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard) and click \"Customers\". Pick a test customer who is IN for this season - their RSVP should say Yes. Note their name, and note whether they are on any upcoming route (open \"Routes and Schedule\" and look for them on a day that has not happened yet).\n2. Click \"Quote Requests\" in the left menu and raise a re-quote for that same test customer, or open an existing quote that is already linked to them.\n3. Send that quote to yourself by email.\n4. Open the email as if you were the customer and press the button that declines it.\n5. Go back into admin and look at that customer in \"All Customers\".\n6. Click \"Warehouse and Customer Numbers\" and look at the recycle list.\n7. Go back to \"Routes and Schedule\" and look at the upcoming day they were on.\n8. Click \"Customer Messages\" and open the \"System\" folder.","The customer's RSVP should now say No - not Yes, and not blank. They should appear on the warehouse recycle list, because their lights are coming back. They should NOT be showing as scheduled, and they should be gone from the upcoming route they were on (a route in the PAST must be left exactly as it was - that is a record of what the crew actually did). There should be one new note in the System folder saying they declined, naming them, and saying their lights are flagged for recycling and how many routes they came off. If they only meant to turn down an addition rather than the whole season, that note tells you how to put it back.",1,"","","New, 2026-08-21 - this was a real hole. Declining a quote used to file the quote away and touch NOTHING else: the customer kept reading as a Yes, was never recycled, and stayed on any route already built, so a crew could drive to a house that had said no. The automated suite now runs the whole decline against a fake database and proves every one of those. What it cannot prove is that the real email button, on your real records, reaches the right customer - which matters here more than usual, because a shared phone number in your book is often two different households."],
  [209,"Admin","An outstanding add-on rides along with the RSVP","\u26a0 USE A TEST CUSTOMER, and do this on a test email address of your own.\n1. Log into admin and click \"Quote Requests\". Raise a re-quote for a test customer and choose \"Something added to the house\" as what changed. Type a short note like \"wants the back garage doing as well\".\n2. Price that re-quote and leave it - do NOT send it yet.\n3. Click \"Automation\" in the left menu, then \"Automation Emails\".\n4. Pick your RSVP template, and in the preview picker choose that same test customer. Read the preview to the bottom.\n5. Now choose a DIFFERENT test customer who has no add-on outstanding, and read that preview to the bottom too.\n6. Send the RSVP to yourself and open it on a phone as well as a computer.\n7. In the email, press the button that turns the extra lights down.\n8. Go back into admin and look at that customer in \"All Customers\", and at \"Customer Messages\".","The first preview ends with a shaded box headed \"One more thing - the extra lights you asked about\", showing your note, the price their season WOULD be against what it is now, and two buttons: \"Yes, add it\" and \"No thanks - just my usual lights\". Underneath it must say that saying no only turns down the extra lights and does not change their answer above. The second customer, with nothing outstanding, gets NO box at all - not an empty one. The real email must match the preview exactly and be readable on a phone. After pressing no: the customer must still be in for the season (RSVP still Yes, still scheduled, still on their route, NOT on the recycle list) and there should be a System note saying they turned down the add-on and that this is not a cancellation.",1,"","","New, 2026-08-21. The automated suite proves the box is built for the right customer, that a shared phone number cannot put one household's quote in another's email, and that the preview and the real send produce the same thing. What it cannot prove is that the box survives your email software and looks right on a phone, or that a real customer pressing a real button ends up in the right state - which matters here because the whole point is that no to a garage is NOT no to the season."]
];